2026-08-16 12:19:38,981 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'park-automation-module', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'MEASURED 2026-08-14 — THE SINGLE MOST LOAD-BEARING FACT FOR THIS DESIGN.\n\nPeter, 2026-08-14, when asked whether verbs arrive by DM or from a dashboard page:\n"DM no, BC on Private Channels"\n\nSo commands arrive as BROADCASTS on the Private channel, not as DMs. That makes the\nchannel index safety-critical, and it CANNOT be a configured number:\n\n  curl localhost:8001/<addr>/channels\n\n  E9:B0:3F:17:27:91    idx 0 PRIMARY  (unnamed)\n                       idx 1 SECONDARY \'mqtt\'\n                       idx 2 SECONDARY \'Private\'      <- Private is TWO\n\n  F4:12:FA:39:F7:B6    idx 0 PRIMARY  \'Public\'\n                       idx 1 SECONDARY \'Private\'      <- Private is ONE\n\nTHE SAME NAME IS A DIFFERENT INDEX ON EACH RADIO, and index 1 — the value anyone\nwould reach for — is \'mqtt\' on one of them. An automation that hard-codes channel 1\nas Private would broadcast every reply to the MQTT channel from that radio. That is\nthe concrete, avoidable disaster this note exists to prevent.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THE DESIGN: the Private index must be RESOLVED BY NAME, per radio,\nat both listen time and transmit time. node-dash already caches exactly this —\nws-relay.js `refreshDeviceChannels()` fetches /<addr>/channels on every READY and\nkeeps {index, name, role} per radio in `lastDeviceChannels`, filtered to\nPRIMARY/SECONDARY. Use that; do not re-fetch and do not store a number.\n\nThe `messages` table already carries both halves of the pair needed to identify the\nchannel a command arrived on: `channel` (the per-gateway INDEX) and `device` (the\nradio that heard it). Neither alone is sufficient.\n\nWHAT ALREADY EXISTS AND SHOULD BE REUSED RATHER THAN REBUILT:\n  src/probe-plan.js     a CLOSED verb vocabulary + named plans, DB-backed, served\n                        and validated over GET/PUT /probe-plans. Already the\n                        "configurable without touching the file" model he asked for.\n  src/callout.js        the guard model: a hard transmit ceiling in code that config\n                        can only LOWER, guards that must explain their own silence,\n                        ships disabled by default.\n  src/mission-runner.js runs the step sequences.\n  src/mesh-send.js      sendMeshText({gatewayNodeId, text, channel, to, reply_id}).\n\nTHE ONLY REAL GAP: nothing parses an inbound text as a command. Verified across\nsrc/bridge-events.js, src/alerts.js and src/persist.js — an inbound TEXT_MESSAGE_APP\nis decoded, stored, alerted on and broadcast to the browser, and that is all.\n\nSTILL OPEN FOR PETER: who may issue a verb. A broadcast on Private is readable and\nwritable by everyone on that channel, so "accept from anyone on Private" and "accept\nonly from an allowlist of Peter\'s own nodes" are different products. Do not assume.'}
