2026-08-16 12:31:29,740 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'private-channel-resolver', 'title': 'GAP - one global resolver picks the private channel by shared key; channel index must never be assumed', 'steps': ['Investigate: every place that assumes a channel index or name, and what the bridge exposes per radio', "Document: the resolver's contract, where the designated key lives, and the psk-never-stored rule", 'Implement', 'Observe: prove it resolves 2 on one radio and 1 on the other, and that removing it breaks the check', 'Track']}
2026-08-16 12:31:48,320 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'private-channel-resolver', 'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'PETER, VERBATIM, 2026-08-14:\n\n"so it it does not exist it should be a global func, that selects the private channel based on a shared key (which may be in a config file) you CANNOT use channel index and assume that channel 1 is the private channel on all devices. a commmand verb would be prefixed by \\"@\\" i.e \\"@xxxx\\""\n\n(The "@" half is the automation module\'s, not this task\'s — recorded on\npark-automation-module. This task is the resolver he says must exist first.)\n\nIT DOES NOT EXIST. Verified 2026-08-14. The nearest thing is the send form\'s\nchannel dropdown, `public/app-messages.js:49-53`, which reads `dev.channels` off\nthe device_list. `src/ws-relay.js` `refreshDeviceChannels()` builds that list as\n`{index, name, role}` and DISCARDS the psk, so nothing downstream can match by key.\n\nWHY INDEX CANNOT BE USED — MEASURED, `GET localhost:8001/<addr>/channels`,\n2026-08-14. Fingerprint = first 12 hex of sha256(psk); no key printed:\n\n  E9:B0:3F:17:27:91   idx 0 PRIMARY   name=None      psk_fp=5d4dc0ce1ab5\n                      idx 1 SECONDARY name=mqtt      psk_fp=None  (unencrypted)\n                      idx 2 SECONDARY name=Private   psk_fp=1ee0ab2c7a2b\n  F4:12:FA:39:F7:B6   idx 0 PRIMARY   name=Public    psk_fp=5d4dc0ce1ab5\n                      idx 1 SECONDARY name=Private   psk_fp=1ee0ab2c7a2b\n\n  INDEX FAILS  Private is 2 on one radio, 1 on the other. Index 1 on E9:B0 is\n               `mqtt` — unencrypted, and the one channel that is emphatically NOT\n               private. Anything hard-coding 1 broadcasts to MQTT from that radio.\n  NAME FAILS   and this is the part I had wrong in my own first proposal: the two\n               PRIMARYs are the SAME channel by psk, but one is named `Public` and\n               the other is UNNAMED. A name is not an identity.\n  PSK WORKS    the fingerprint matches across radios in both cases.\n\nSO: a channel\'s identity is its KEY. One channel = one psk, at whatever index, on\nhowever many radios, named whatever.\n\nTHE DESIGNATED KEY MUST BE CONFIGURED, not inferred. "Shared across both radios"\ndoes NOT single out Private — PRIMARY is shared too. Peter: "(which may be in a\nconfig file)". So config names WHICH key is the private one.\n\nSTORE THE FINGERPRINT, NEVER THE KEY. `psk` is already in\n`public/app-forms.js` SENSITIVE_FIELDS, and docs/modules/browser-playwright-audit.md\nallows only `psk_present` and a length in artifacts. A sha256 fingerprint is\nsufficient to match and safe to persist and log.\n\nKNOWN CONSUMER DEFECT THIS UNBLOCKS (Domain 2, its own task):\n`public/app-messages.js:34` classifies traffic as private with\n`privateChannel: (r.channel ?? 0) !== 0`. On E9:B0 that labels the `mqtt` channel\nprivate. It is mislabelling live traffic today.'}
2026-08-16 12:34:02,731 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_file_attach args={'file_path': 'docs/modules/channels.md', 'kind': 'plan', 'label': 'channels spec — PSK-fingerprint channel identity'}
