2026-08-16 12:50:06,956 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'automation-verb-listener', 'title': 'GAP - @verb commands broadcast on the Private channel, acted on and answered', 'steps': ["Investigate: the rotator point/hold API, the traceroute entry point, where an inbound packet's rssi/snr/hops live, and the registration pattern", 'Document: the verb table, the guards, and the reply contract', 'Implement', 'Observe: a real @verb over the air, and the guards proven by removal', 'Track']}
2026-08-16 12:51:13,809 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'automation-verb-listener', 'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'PETER, 2026-08-14: verbs are prefixed "@" — "a commmand verb would be prefixed by\n\\"@\\" i.e \\"@xxxx\\"" — and arrive as BROADCASTS on the Private channel, not DMs:\n"DM no, BC on Private Channels".\n\nWHO MAY COMMAND — HIS DECISION, RECORDED SO IT IS NOT "FIXED" BACK. I proposed\nrestricting verbs to his own nodes, because a broadcast on Private is writable by\nanyone on that channel and these verbs move hardware. He overruled it:\n\n  "no need to restrict from my nodes, this is a provate channel. I am not\n   bothered about that at this point."\n\nSo ANY sender on the Private channel may issue a verb. That is deliberate, it is\nhis call, and the concern was raised and declined. "at this point" is his own\nhedge — if an allowlist is wanted later the hook belongs in one place, so the\nsender check is a single function that currently returns true rather than being\nabsent entirely.\n\nTHE DRIVING USE CASE: a remote survey. He was in a field, pinged home 11 times,\nmoving an antenna between pings. One person, no operator at the dashboard.\n\nPHASE 1 FINDINGS — the pieces, all verified 2026-08-14:\n\n  rotator.point(az, {timeoutMs, hold, patienceMs, by})   src/rotator.js:297\n      Already cooperative for the SHARED yagi: returns {ok, busy, holdMs,\n      retryInMs, timeout}, and waits out another user\'s stated hold rather than\n      fighting it. `by` labels the holder. rotator.hold(ms) is separate.\n  traceroute.run(target, opts)                            src/traceroute.js:169\n      Owns its own aiming via opts.aim — @route must NOT aim separately, or it\n      repeats the double-aim defect already in the ledger.\n  bearing(lat1, lon1, lat2, lon2)                         src/utils.js:14\n      With nodeList.homePos for the origin — what @point needs.\n  sendMeshText({gatewayNodeId, text, channel, to, reply_id})  src/mesh-send.js:51\n      The single recording transmit path. Every send goes through it.\n  isPrivate(device, index) / privateIndexFor(device)       src/channels.js\n      Shipped in 15d5804. The gate and the reply slot.\n  packet fields                                            src/persist.js:415-445\n      rx_rssi, rx_snr, channel, from, id, hop_start/hop_limit -> hops.\n\nTHE TRAP THAT WILL BITE IF IGNORED — BOTH RADIOS HEAR THE SAME BROADCAST.\nMeasured in the live store:\n\n  select packet_id, count(*) n, group_concat(device) from messages\n    where ts > now-86400 and is_dm=0 group by packet_id having n>1;\n  -> 3049001458 | 2 | F4:12:FA:39:F7:B6,E9:B0:3F:17:27:91\n\nSo one "@point" would fire the handler TWICE — two rotator moves and two replies —\nunless deduped by packet_id. ws-relay already does this for its own broadcast path\n(`_seenLivePktIds`), which is precedent, not something to share: that set is\ncleared on bridge reconnect for its own reasons.\n\nREGISTRATION: from registerBridgeEvents (src/bridge-events.js:14), mirroring\nhandleAlertEvent. index.js:56 warns that anything registered after attachWsRelay\nsilently never fires — registerBridgeEvents is called at index.js:518, after\nattachWsRelay at :399, and works, so that is the proven slot.\n\nDEFERRED, AND SAY SO RATHER THAN PRETEND: when both radios hear a @ping, the\nper-radio signal comparison is exactly what a survey wants ("OMNI -95/6.5,\nYAGI -88/8.0"). Doing it needs a coalescing window, and /derive forbids picking a\n"safely large" wait. v1 replies once from the radio that heard it first and names\nthat radio, so the number is never ambiguous about which antenna produced it.'}
2026-08-16 12:54:55,204 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_file_attach args={'file_path': 'docs/modules/automation.md', 'kind': 'plan', 'label': 'automation spec — @verb listener on the Private channel'}
2026-08-16 13:05:05,429 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_switch args={'name': 'automation-verb-listener'}
2026-08-16 13:05:18,757 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'automation-verb-listener', 'kind': 'plan', 'text': 'SHIPPED 8cdbba4. src/automation.js + one call from src/bridge-events.js, after\nhandleEvent so the command is logged before it is acted on.\n\nVERBS (hard-coded contents, configurable SHAPE): @point [!node], @ping, @route\n[!node]. Argument optional, defaults to the SENDER — in a field, one-handed, the\nnode you want pointed at is you.\n\nPROVEN OVER THE AIR, live, not with fixtures:\n  @ping  -> "12:57 pong YAGI -30dBm 6.8dB 0hop 94%", reply_id = the command\n  @point -> "13:02 point: yagi at 202deg, holding 30s"; rotator az 105 -> 201,\n            held=true, holdMs=29738\n  Sent on OMNI\'s private slot (2), heard on YAGI\'s private slot (1) — different\n  index, same channel, resolved by key.\n  Disabled: command arrives, ZERO replies.\n\nTWO DEFECTS THE LIVE TEST FOUND AND A FIXTURE WOULD NOT HAVE:\n  1. position is `position.latitude_i` (int degrees x 1e7), NOT lat/lon. The\n     first @point answered "no position" for a node we plainly held one for.\n  2. a third party on the private channel sent "@claude" — human chatter that\n     merely began with "@" — and the first build broadcast the usage string at\n     the whole channel. Unknown verbs are now SILENT, refusal recorded on a hook.\n\nSTATE ON PETER\'S INSTANCE: automation.enabled = TRUE in config (he asked for this\nurgently and wants to use it). The CODE default is false, so a fresh install\nstill ships silent.\n\nNOT DONE, deliberately:\n  - no scheduling / sequences. A survey IS a sequence; mission-runner.js already\n    runs step sequences and is where that belongs.\n  - no name lookup — arguments are !hex or omitted.\n  - no per-radio signal comparison. It is what a survey most wants ("OMNI -95/6.5\n    vs YAGI -88/8.0") but needs a coalescing window, and /derive forbids picking\n    a "safely large" wait. A reply names the radio that heard it instead.\n  - no test in tests/. The proof above is a session transcript, not a repeatable\n    check — same class as the watchdog defect already in the ledger.'}
