2026-08-16 13:07:15,348 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'boundary-doc-states-fixed-violation', 'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'docs/ALARM_TRANSPORT_BOUNDARY.md asserted, in the PRESENT TENSE, a violation that\ncommit 21e2432 had already deleted:\n\n  "applyAlignModel() in src/alarm-host.js currently violates this by calling\n   dashMode.set(0) to force PASV for an align session."\n\nIt does not. Verified 2026-08-14:\n  grep -n dashMode src/alarm-host.js   -> one COMMENT at :295, no call\n  grep -rn "dashMode.set" src/*.js     -> lifecycle.js:20,:37 and rotator-api.js:90\n                                          (core, legitimate); NO alarm file\n\nWHY THIS ONE MATTERS MORE THAN AN ORDINARY STALE DOC: CLAUDE.md names this file as\nmandatory reading before touching anything alarm-shaped. A reader is told a live\nviolation exists in code that is already correct — so the next session either\n"fixes" working code, or trusts the doc over the source somewhere it matters more.\n\nFOUND during the /handover read audit, not carried from any handover — it was in\nneither. Ledger step 93.\n\nFIX: rewritten as history rather than deleted. The rule it illustrates (modes use\nthe pointer, they must not control or gate it) is still the rule, and the fact it\nwas broken once from the plugin\'s end is exactly why the boundary doc exists. The\npassage now carries the grep to re-run, so the next reader checks rather than\ntrusts — the same standard this doc demands of everything else.\n\napplyAlignModel() itself still exists and still returns early (section `align` is\nok:false while park-align-ownership is undecided). The doc keeps the instruction\nto DELETE rather than move it, because carrying dead code across a boundary is how\na violation gets reinstated by accident.'}
