2026-08-16 13:10:34,383 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_new args={'task': 'bugs', 'title': 'BUG - the read-path contract check misses any config key read through a const', 'description': 'CONFIRMED 2026-08-14, found while adding two config keys.\n\n`scripts/check_contract.mjs` has a check "config keys read but with no read path",\nwhich requires any key read by code to be declared in config-api DEFAULTS or given\na route. It works — it caught `automation.enabled` and\n`automation.max_replies_per_hour` the moment they were added, and its own comment\nhistory shows it previously caught `node_filters.msg_only` and\n`node_filters.hide_mqtt_only`, both real user-visible defects.\n\nBUT IT ONLY SEES STRING LITERALS. In the same commit, `src/channels.js` read\n\n    getConfig(PRIVATE_FP_KEY, null)      // PRIVATE_FP_KEY = \'channels.private_psk_fp\'\n\nand the check did NOT flag it, because the key is a const rather than a literal at\nthe call site. Both keys were equally undeclared; only the literal one was caught.\n\nWHY THIS MATTERS MORE THAN THE ONE KEY: a const is the tidier way to write it, so\nthe checker is weakest exactly where the code is best. And `DEFAULTS` gates BOTH\nread and write, so an undeclared key silently 404s on save and reverts on reload —\nwhich is precisely the class of defect this check exists to catch.\n\nBOTH KEYS ARE NOW DECLARED, so this is about the checker, not about a live fault.\n\nPOSSIBLE FIX, not attempted: resolve single-assignment `const NAME = \'...\'` in the\nsame module before matching, or require the key to be declared where the const is\ndefined. Neither is free — a general resolver is a small static analyser.\n\nDO NOT "fix" this by banning consts for config keys. The const is the better code.'}
