2026-08-16 13:14:15,441 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_notes_set args={'number': 87, 'text': 'SEVERITY RAISED, 2026-08-14. This is WORSE than recorded, not better.\n\nThe entry says "303 of 326 YAGI traceroutes in 24h had aim_mode=\'none\'" (93%).\nRe-measured just now over the last 24 h:\n\n  select tx_device, aim_mode, count(*) n, count(distinct rotator_az) az_values\n    from traceroute_history where ts > strftime(\'%s\',\'now\')-86400\n    group by tx_device, aim_mode;\n\n  tx_device            aim_mode   n     distinct rotator_az\n  (none)               (null)       5   0\n  E9:B0:3F:17:27:91    none       355   0      <- OMNI, omnidirectional, fine\n  F4:12:FA:39:F7:B6    none       636   60     <- YAGI\n\n636 of 636. ONE HUNDRED PERCENT, not 93%. Every single YAGI traceroute in the last\n24 hours went out with aim_mode=\'none\'.\n\nAND THE 60 DISTINCT AZIMUTHS ARE THE POINT. The beam was not parked at one\nunhelpful bearing — it was wherever the scanner, the alarm or a previous mission\nhad last left it, and 636 shots went out across 60 different directions with no\nrelationship to their targets. A 35-degree beam aimed at random.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR ANALYSIS, not just for reach: every one of those 636 rows records\na result that cannot be attributed to a bearing. Any conclusion drawn from YAGI\ntraceroute outcomes in this period is confounded, in the same way the 120-degree\nboom offset invalidated everything before 2026-08-05 (see the beam-offset-epoch\nentry). That is two overlapping epochs of unusable beam evidence.\n\nNOT FIXED — audit only, and node-dash was not restarted for it. Peter was testing\nthe automation remotely at the time and a restart would have broken his session.'}
2026-08-16 13:14:26,445 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_notes_set args={'number': 74, 'text': 'DISPROVED WITH EVIDENCE, 2026-08-14. Not a latent ReferenceError.\n\nThe entry says app-perf.js "calls persistSet() with no import". The first half is\ntrue, the conclusion is not:\n\n  grep persistSet public/app-perf.js     -> :83 :84 :106 :133 :321 :429, bare calls\n  grep "^import" public/app-perf.js      -> only { bandFor, toneCls, toneBadge }\n  grep "export function persistSet"      -> public/app-persist.js:187\n  grep "window.persistSet"               -> public/app.js:26  window.persistSet = persistSet\n\nSo it resolves as a GLOBAL, not as an unresolved identifier. Every call site in\napp-perf.js is inside a handler body, and app.js:26 runs at module load, so the\nglobal is assigned long before any of them can fire. There is no ReferenceError.\n\nWHAT IS ACTUALLY TRUE, and is a much smaller thing: app-perf.js is the ONLY module\nusing the window global. Eight others import it properly —\napp-radar, app-devices, app-config, app-nav, app-ws, app-messages, app.js,\napp-observatory. One module relying on a global that exists for a different\nconsumer (app-node-status.js:100) is inconsistent and would break if app.js:26\nwere ever tidied away. That is a TIDY, not a bug.\n\nLEFT OPEN rather than closed, downgraded to TIDY: the fix is a one-line import and\nit removes the coupling. Not done here because it is Domain 2 and unrelated to\nanything else in flight.\n\nCLASS: an absence asserted from a grep — "no import" was read as "not reachable"\nwithout checking whether it was reachable another way. Same shape as the standing\nrule about greps wider or narrower than the claim they support.'}
2026-08-16 13:14:55,741 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_notes_set args={'number': 59, 'text': 'STILL LIVE, re-verified 2026-08-14 — and READING THE CODE ALONE WOULD HAVE SAID\n"FIXED". That is the whole lesson of this re-check.\n\n  src/dash-mode.js:49  MODE_DEFAULTS.pasv = { rx: \'non-rotator\', tx: \'rx\', ... }\n\nThe code default is correct and was corrected in f2bb26d. But defaults are only\ndefaults, and the STORED config overrides them:\n\n  sqlite3 data/node-dash.db "select value from config where key=\'mode_config\';"\n  {"pasv":{"rx":"all","tx":"rx","aim":"none","radio":"auto"},\n   "actv":{"rx":"primary","tx":"rotator","aim":"auto","radio":"auto"},\n   "scan":{"rx":"rotator","tx":"rotator","aim":"none","radio":"auto"},\n   "disc":{"tx":"rotator","rx":"all","aim":"auto","radio":"auto"}}\n\npasv.rx is "all". So the defect the entry describes is exactly as recorded, in the\nlive system, right now — with `tx: \'rx\'` meaning "transmit via whoever heard it",\na passive trace can still put the YAGI on air mid-mission.\n\nTWO MORE DIVERGENCES IN THE SAME BLOB, not previously recorded:\n  disc.rx  = "all"      (same fault, same consequence, in discovery mode)\n  actv.rx  = "primary"  vs the code default \'rotator\' — ACTV is supposed to be\n                        "the aimed YAGI hears and transmits", and the stored value\n                        says something else entirely.\n\nNOT FIXED, and deliberately so: changing mode_config changes WHICH RADIO\nTRANSMITS. Peter was out testing the automation remotely when this was found, and\nan unannounced change to transmit behaviour mid-test would be indistinguishable\nfrom a bug in what he was testing. It is on the decision list for his return.\n\nTHE GENERAL POINT, worth more than this entry: a stored config blob can silently\nreinstate a defect that was fixed in code, and `git log` shows the fix while the\nrunning system has the fault. Verify a mode/config claim against the DB, never\nagainst MODE_DEFAULTS.'}
2026-08-16 13:15:40,598 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_notes_set args={'number': 2, 'text': 'RE-MEASURED 2026-08-14 — WORSE, and the entry names a table that does not exist.\n\nTHE TABLE IS `environment_history`, NOT `env_history`. Steps 1, 2, 3 and 6 all say\n"env_history". There is no such table:\n\n  select name from sqlite_master where type=\'table\' and name like \'%env%\';\n  -> environment_history\n\nThat is not pedantry. A reader who runs the entry\'s own name gets "no such table"\nand can reasonably conclude the data is gone — the false-absence trap this project\nhas been bitten by repeatedly. Whoever works entries 1/2/3/6 must translate first.\n\nTHE NUMBERS, re-measured:\n\n  select sum(c-1) excess_rows, count(*) dupe_groups,\n         (select count(*) from environment_history) total\n    from (select ts,num,count(*) c from environment_history\n            group by ts,num having c>1);\n\n  excess_rows 73250 | dupe_groups 49880 | total 249591\n\n  RECORDED   ~13,571 duplicate rows, 15% of table\n  ACTUAL      73,250 excess rows, 29.3% of table\n\nSo more than FIVE TIMES the recorded excess, and nearly double the proportion. The\nthree-writer SSOT violation in step 1 is still producing them; this is not a\nhistorical backlog that stopped growing.\n\nNOT FIXED — a de-duplicating migration on 250k rows is not something to run\nunattended while Peter is out, and step 1 (the three writers) must be fixed first\nor the duplicates simply regrow. Fixing the symptom before the cause would make\nthe next measurement look like success.'}
2026-08-16 13:15:53,131 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_notes_set args={'number': 67, 'text': 'RE-MEASURED 2026-08-14 — THE BACKFILL WORKED AND THE LEAK IS STILL OPEN.\n\n  select count(*) from nodeinfo ni join nodes n on n.num=ni.num\n    where ni.lat is not null and ni.lat != 0 and (n.lat is null or n.lat = 0);\n  -> 3\n\n  !38295b25  (no short name)  nodeinfo lat 51.564\n  !5deda4aa  (no short name)  nodeinfo lat 52.560\n  !f68e1508  TCm2             nodeinfo lat 50.941\n\nRECORDED HISTORY, for contrast: the entry says 11. The task\n`backfill-stranded-positions` was marked COMPLETE with 2 still stranded, and a\nlater handover said 1 remained. It is now 3.\n\nSO THE COUNT WENT 11 -> 2 -> 1 -> 3. The backfill was a ONE-OFF that drained the\npool; the path that strands them was never closed, so it refills. Anyone reading\n"COMPLETE" on that task and this entry\'s "11" would draw two wrong conclusions:\nthat it is finished, and that it is worse than it is.\n\nTHIS IS THE SHAPE TO WATCH FOR, not the number: a task marked complete because its\nmigration ran, while the defect that made the migration necessary is untouched.\nThe fix is upstream — whatever writes nodeinfo.lat must also reach nodes.lat, or\nnodes.lat must be derived rather than stored twice.\n\nRELATED, same query family: `nodes` now holds ZERO rows at 0,0 (that half of the\nnull-island entry is clean), but `nodeinfo` still holds 31. See step 70.\n\nNOT FIXED — audit only. A backfill is a write to 3 live rows and the upstream leak\nwould refill it; closing the leak is the actual task.'}
2026-08-16 13:16:01,085 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_notes_set args={'number': 70, 'text': 'HALF FIXED, half live. Re-measured 2026-08-14:\n\n  select count(*) from nodes    where lat=0 and lon=0;   ->  0\n  select count(*) from nodeinfo where lat=0 and lon=0;   -> 31\n\nThe entry says "neither nodes nor nodeinfo rejects 0,0". The `nodes` half is now\nclean — zero rows at null island. The `nodeinfo` half is not: 31 rows still sit at\n0,0.\n\nWHY THAT STILL MATTERS EVEN THOUGH `nodes` IS CLEAN: step 67 shows positions flow\nnodeinfo -> nodes and that the flow is leaky. A 0,0 sitting in nodeinfo is a\nplausible-looking coordinate waiting for the next path that copies it forward.\n`nodes` being clean today is not evidence the guard exists; it may only mean\nnothing has copied those 31 yet.\n\nTo close this properly the check is on `_validCoord`, not on a row count — reject\n0,0 at the WRITE, and the count becomes a consequence rather than the target.\n\nNOT FIXED — audit only, and deleting or rewriting 31 live rows unattended is not\nwarranted when the guard is the actual fix.'}
