2026-08-16 14:08:21,061 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'channel-callers-onto-resolver', 'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'Peter, 2026-08-16: "I suspect there are lots of other places where the wrong\nchannel is used. resolve should be the SSOT" — then "yes with /idiot" to migrating\nthe four sites found while making channels.js the SSOT.\n\nPHASE 1 FINDINGS. Two of the four are not what I called them in the channels spec,\nand one blocker turned up that has to be fixed first.\n\n  src/callout.js:85 + :264   GENUINELY CHOOSES. `DEFAULTS.channel = 0` with the\n      comment "PRIMARY. Explicit, never inferred." The INTENT is right — a public\n      callout belongs on the public channel — but 0 is an index, and on the YAGI\n      index 0 is the misnamed `Public` slot. A callout sent from the YAGI today\n      goes nowhere while callout_log records a packet id and the hook reports\n      "called out to X at Nkm". -> indexFor(dev,\'pub\'), refuse when null.\n\n  src/messages-api.js:14     `req.body.channel ?? 0`. An API call that omits the\n      channel SILENTLY GOES OUT ON THE PUBLIC MESH. This is the dangerous one:\n      the browser form always supplies it, so the default only fires for a caller\n      that forgot — which is exactly when you least want a public broadcast.\n      -> refuse with 400 rather than defaulting.\n\n  src/mesh-send.js:28, :52   `channel ?? 0` in the recording, and `const body =\n      { text, channel }` — JSON.stringify DROPS an undefined key, so the gateway\n      receives no channel and applies its own default of 0. Every sender funnels\n      through here, so this is the chokepoint. -> require an explicit channel,\n      throw otherwise.\n\n  src/imap-receiver.js:112   I OVER-CALLED THIS ONE. `ctx.channel ?? 0` reads\n      from a reply token created in alerts.js:169/:185 with `pkt.channel ?? 0` —\n      it ECHOES the channel the original message arrived on. That is the same\n      shape as alerts.js and persist.js, which I correctly excluded. It is not a\n      choosing site and does not need the resolver.\n\nBLOCKER FOUND, AND IT IS MINE FROM AN HOUR AGO: the resolver\'s table is keyed by\nMAC (ws-relay stores `addr.toUpperCase()`), but callout is handed a `!hex`:\n\n  callout-runner.js:84   const gatewayNodeId = transmitterForMode(mode, {})\n  dash-mode.js:236       return macToNodeId(mac)          <- !hex, not a MAC\n\nSo `indexFor(\'!fa39f7b4\',\'pub\')` returns null today and callout would simply stop\nsending. This is the exact failure `traceroute.js:_recordFailure` already carries a\ncomment about — "a node_id-side compare can never match a MAC-vocabulary device,\nwhich silently skipped the az stamp".\n\nDECISION: the SSOT absorbs the vocabulary, callers do not convert. ws-relay knows\nboth halves and will register the table under the MAC and the node_id. Pushing\nconversion onto every caller is how this class of bug keeps recurring.'}
