2026-08-17 08:36:49,396 1496996 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'flow-chart-before-spec', 'title': 'GAP - the decision logic is invisible; draw it by hand before deciding whether to generate it', 'steps': ['DOCUMENT - draw closure_of, verdict_of and hook in mermaid, read line by line from the code', 'VERIFY - check every drawn path terminates: no undetermined decision, no unnamed fall-through', 'DECIDE - Peter looks at it and rules: does this make the logic legible enough to automate?', 'DISCUSS - the yaml structure, given a flow is a graph and yaml nesting is a tree', 'TOOL - generate the yaml from the code and diff it against the hand-drawn charts']}
2026-08-17 08:36:59,280 1496996 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'WHY, in Peter\'s words: "all code including this is primarily based on logic and decisions and actions from decisions. all of this is buried in the code and I have no real way of realistically seeing that. the func spec describes in words generically what the software does, but a flow diagram is explicit."\n\nAnd: "something that is missing from every single project is that I cannot look at the code and see the flow."\n\nHis instinct on order — draw one BY HAND first, before any generator: "until I can see something I dont know whether it will work."\n\nThree further propositions of his, recorded because they change the shape of the work if they hold:\n1. A functional spec should be DERIVED FROM the flow chart, not written beside it.\n2. The flow chart becomes the FIRST document in any project. Existing projects get one retrospectively.\n3. "a flow chart should never have an undetermined path or decision."\n\n(3) is the load-bearing one — it is mechanically checkable, and it is exactly the defect that occurred in this repo on 2026-08-17. `closure_of`\'s mtime branch overloaded a single `None` return to mean BOTH "cannot answer" and "no". That undetermined path is what made eleven proven entries unclosable, and my first fix made it worse by letting a weaker rung overturn a definite refusal. A chart forbidding undetermined paths forces both meanings to be named as separate edges.\n\nOn yaml as the source: "the fact that yaml is indented to me seems the right data source for a flow chart." Recorded as his instinct, with the caveat for the DISCUSS step below.\n\nScope: three functions — closure_of, verdict_of, hook. NOT the program. The moment it charts `add` or `list` it has become a documentation generator, which is the second-system failure that killed specboard.'}
2026-08-17 08:37:15,167 1496996 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'plan', 'text': 'FIVE PHASES, GATED AT PHASE 3. Nothing after it is built until Peter has looked and ruled.\n\nPhase DOCUMENT — draw closure_of, verdict_of and hook in mermaid, reading each function line by line from the source. NOT from memory: a chart drawn from recollection is the worst of both worlds, since it is both wrong and confident. Mermaid because it is plain text, in the repo, diffable, renders anywhere — and because if a generator ever follows, the target format is already fixed and the hand-drawn chart becomes its first test case.\n  Deliverable: docs/flow/*.mmd, DATED and marked UNMAINTAINED. Without that mark a probe silently becomes the documentation, which is the one failure mode a probe has.\n\nPhase VERIFY — apply Peter\'s rule: no undetermined path, no unnamed fall-through. Every decision node must resolve every branch to a named node or a terminal. Any place the code overloads one return value with two meanings is a finding, not a drawing problem.\n\nPhase DECIDE — Peter\'s gate. Does the picture make the logic legible? If no, STOP: the generator cannot rescue an unreadable chart, and we have spent an afternoon rather than a week finding that out.\n\nPhase DISCUSS — the yaml structure, and one caveat that must be settled before any schema:\n  A FLOW IS A GRAPH; YAML NESTING IS A TREE. Paths converge and rejoin — two branches reach the same terminal. Pure indentation cannot express convergence without duplicating a subtree, and a duplicated subtree drifts. So nodes need IDS and edges reference them BY ID; indentation can carry readability but not identity.\n  Also to settle: does the spec really derive from the chart? The chart holds decisions. It does NOT hold purpose, and it does not hold WHAT IT DOES NOT DO — an absence is not a node, and that section is the most valuable half of docs/SPEC.md.\n\nPhase TOOL — generate the yaml from the code and diff against the hand-drawn charts. DIRECTION IS FIXED: code is truth, yaml is a derived committed artefact (a lockfile for the decision logic), diagram renders from yaml. A check fails when the committed yaml no longer matches the code. Hand-written yaml checked against code is specboard\'s 377 claims and drifts silently; yaml as source with generated code is a DSL and a compiler. Both rejected.\n\nPRIORITY: this sits BEHIND the three bugs raised by the audit — hook-prints-an-invocation-that-does-not-work, add-accepts-paths-that-do-not-resolve, an-entry-with-no-files-can-never-close. Peter: "cli-styling is not urgent, what is urgent is that what we already have is bug free." Phases DOCUMENT and DECIDE are cheap and answer a question, so they may run first; TOOL may not.'}
