2026-08-17 22:59:58,118 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'REMOVING THE ROUTE DID NOT REMOVE THE PATH. IT MADE IT UNMEDIATED.\n\nMeasured 2026-08-17 22:58 BST, immediately after `traceroute-callers-rewrite`\ndeleted node-dash\'s `POST /:nodeId/traceroute`:\n\n  curl -X POST localhost:8000/%212687afb1/traceroute -d \'{"to":646426545}\'\n  -> HTTP 200\n\nNot 404. src/index.js:379\n\n  app.use(/^\\/![0-9a-f]+/i, proxyToBridge);\n\nis a CATCH-ALL that forwards any `/!hex/...` path straight to mesh-gw. With the\ntraceroute router\'s POST handler gone, the request no longer matches it at line\n350 and falls through to the proxy at 379 instead. mesh-gw accepted it and\nreturned 200.\n\nSo the two browser callers are NOT the dead 404 buttons the parent task recorded:\n\n  public/app-perf.js:80     fetchJSON(`/${target}/traceroute`, \'POST\', ...)\n  public/app-radar.js:1233  fetchJSON(`/${nodeId}/traceroute`, \'POST\', {})\n\nThey still put frames on air. What they have LOST is every guard traceroute.js\nprovided, all of which the parent task\'s goal note lists as scar tissue from real\nincidents:\n\n  - no cooldown, so a held button is an unthrottled transmitter\n  - no point()-before-send, so it can transmit mid-slew\n  - no beam_deg stamped from the transmitting radio\n  - no send_failed / timeout distinction\n  - no row in traceroute_history at all — the probe above created NONE, so these\n    transmissions are now INVISIBLE to the dashboard that caused them\n\nThat last one is the worst of it: node-dash can transmit and have no record it\ndid. "Zero traceroutes dispatched" measured from traceroute_history would read\nas success while the buttons were still firing.\n\nI CAUSED ONE OF THESE MYSELF. The curl above was my own probe, sent expecting a\n404. mesh-gw returned 200, so it was accepted and I must assume it went on air.\nI did not choose its channel — the proxy passes the request through and mesh-gw\npicks — so I cannot claim it stayed off PRIMARY. Recorded here rather than left\nin a terminal, because an unlogged transmission is exactly the failure this task\nis about.\n\nWHY THIS IS ITS OWN TASK: CLAUDE.md — "No fix may span domains in a single\ntask." The removals are Domain 2 (public/). The parent task is Domain 1.\n\nDO NOT "FIX" THIS BY RESTORING THE ROUTE. Peter, 2026-08-17: "no\nre-implementation logic will be decided until all existing calls to it are\ncompletely removed from code." The callers go; the route stays gone.\n\nWORTH DECIDING SEPARATELY, NOT HERE: whether that catch-all proxy should refuse\nPOST at all. It means node-dash forwards ANY unknown per-device verb to mesh-gw\nunexamined — traceroute is simply the one we found. That is a Domain 1 question\nabout index.js:379 and belongs in its own task.'}
