2026-08-17 23:43:21,679 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'traceroute-calls-resolve-and-aim', 'title': 'DISCUSS - traceroute must CALL resolve and aim, not re-implement them; three functions, independently callable', 'steps': ['Investigate: does traceroute.js resolve the radio and aim the rotator itself instead of calling out?', 'Document: what moves to resolve, what moves to aim, what is left in traceroute', 'Implement: the moves, one module at a time', 'Observe: each function works called alone, and traceroute works calling them', 'Track']}
2026-08-17 23:43:42,353 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'PETER, 2026-08-17, verbatim:\n\n  "so using SSOT, aim, resolve and traceroute are 3 functions that may be called\n   independently as well as in the case of traceroute dependently. traceroute\n   should call and not duplicate what the other functions provide. so there\n   needs to be changes not only to traceroute, but also to resolve and maybe\n   aim. investigate whether traceroute as it stands now breaks this rule and\n   attempts to resolve and aim itself rather than calling the funcs to do that."\n\nAnd, setting the design earlier in the same conversation:\n\n  "traceroute needs min 2 args. 1. the node to trace. 2. the radio to use. if\n   the requested radio (device) is defined as omnidirectional i.e beam width 360\n   degrees then no further discovery is needed. if it is not then the device\'s\n   rotator needs to be resolved. so if it is directional the device\'s rotator\n   needs to aim the antenna, either at the node directly, or if it is relayed at\n   its first hop node... the resolver should be a SSOT that determines and\n   provides this info."\n\nTHE RULE THIS TASK ENFORCES: three functions — resolve, aim, traceroute. Each\ncallable on its own. traceroute CALLS the other two; it does not re-implement\nthem.\n\nSTARTING STATE, established 2026-08-17 and not to be re-derived:\n\n  - Everything that CALLED traceroute is gone (commits 2ef5611, 86400ab,\n    c297b9d, eec402d). src/traceroute.js itself is intact and unwired, by\n    Peter\'s decision. So this task edits a file nothing executes — which is\n    exactly the safe moment to restructure it.\n\n  - THERE IS NO AIM MODULE. `ls src/ | grep -i aim` returns nothing; the\n    rotator-facing files are rotator.js, rotator-api.js,\n    rotator-config-schema.js. So "aim" is a module that must be CREATED, not\n    refactored. Peter said "maybe aim" — it is not maybe, it does not exist.\n\n  - THE RESOLVER IS src/channels.js AND IT ONLY KNOWS CHANNELS. Its exports are\n    ROLES, ROLE_CONFIG_KEY, channelKey, setDeviceChannels, channelsFor,\n    designatedKey, indexFor, roleOf, isRole, resolveChannel, divergences.\n    Nothing about directionality or rotators.\n\n  - THE CAPABILITY DATA ALREADY EXISTS, in device-config.js DEFAULTS:\n    `is_rotator` (:34) and `beam_deg` (:38, default 360). Live values read\n    2026-08-17: OMNI is_rotator=False beam_deg=360; YAGI is_rotator=True\n    beam_deg=35. TWO FIELDS FOR ONE FACT — they agree today and nothing makes\n    them. Peter\'s "beam width 360 = omni" test says beam_deg is the truth.\n\n  - TRACEROUTE TAKES NO CHANNEL. mesh-gw exposes `send_traceroute(to: int)` —\n    `to` only (docs/gw/modules/ble_device.md:66) — whereas send_text validates\n    channel 0-7 (docs/gw/modules/methods.md:21). So the resolver\'s CHANNEL half\n    is unused by traceroute; what traceroute needs from it is radio identity and\n    capability. Consequence not yet answered: if we cannot choose the channel,\n    the firmware does, and if it picks PRIMARY then every traceroute node-dash\n    ever sent went out on the public mesh. That is an mt-transport/mesh-gw\n    question, to be asked of the DEVICE not their docs, and it is NOT this\n    task\'s to answer.\n\nTHE BOUNDARY, from Peter 2026-08-06 and still in force: the mode SSOT owns radio\nroles but must NOT control the pointer. Same here — resolve may report WHICH\nrotator serves a radio and how to reach it; it must never aim. Aiming lives in\none module because the rotator is SHARED with the garage alarm and hold/release\nneeds exactly one owner.\n\nSTEP 1 IS READ-ONLY. Peter asked for an investigation of whether traceroute\nbreaks the rule today. Do not restructure anything until the answer is on the\ntable and he has chosen.'}
2026-08-17 23:44:55,085 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'note', 'name': 'bugs', 'text': 'Four findings from `traceroute-calls-resolve-and-aim` Phase 1, 2026-08-17. All in\nsrc/traceroute.js, which is currently UNWIRED (nothing calls it), so none is live\ntoday — but all four are why the rewrite must not start from this file as-is.\n\n── traceroute-omni-branch-can-return-the-rotator ── CONFIRMED, real defect\n\nsrc/traceroute.js:217-222, in _resolveRadio():\n\n    if (radio === \'omni\') {\n      const tx = transmitterForMode(modeKey);\n      return (rot && tx && tx.toUpperCase() === rot.toUpperCase()) ? tx : tx;\n    }\n\nBOTH ARMS OF THE TERNARY RETURN `tx`. Asking for the omni returns whatever the\nmode\'s transmitter is, which may be the YAGI — the exact radio the caller asked\nnot to use. The condition computes a comparison and discards it.\n\nIts own comment says "we cannot infer the other one here without device config;\nfall back rather than guess" — but `getDeviceCfg` is imported in this same file\nat line 31. The information needed was already in scope.\n\nNot live only because nothing calls run(). Fixed by the resolver work, or by\ndeleting the branch with the rest of _resolveRadio.\n\n── shared-rotator-hold-ceiling-lives-in-the-caller ── CONFIRMED\n\nsrc/traceroute.js:425-431 clamps the rotator hold to\nDISCOVERY_LIMITS.hold_sec[1] * 1000 before calling rotator.point().\n\nThe ceiling exists because Peter, 2026-08-12: "the rotator has many users, not\njust node dash... it is not permitted to issue very long hold periods to\neffectively lock out everything else". But it is enforced in TRACEROUTE, so any\nother caller of rotator.point() is unclamped. src/rotator.js:297 accepts\n`hold = 0` with no upper bound of its own.\n\nPeter already ruled on exactly this, and the ruling is QUOTED IN THIS FILE at\n:385-388: "that is an issue with the node-dash pointer code and not really\ntraceroute. traceroute calls the pointer code, it is the one that needs fixing."\nThe code then implements it in traceroute anyway.\n\nNOT YET KNOWN: whether any other caller currently passes a hold. Not checked.\n\n── directionality-is-two-fields-that-can-disagree ── CONFIRMED\n\ndevice-config.js DEFAULTS carries BOTH `is_rotator` (:34) and `beam_deg` (:38,\ndefault 360) — two independent fields for one fact. Live values read 2026-08-17:\nOMNI is_rotator=False beam_deg=360, YAGI is_rotator=True beam_deg=35. They agree\ntoday; nothing enforces it.\n\ntraceroute consumes them SEPARATELY and for different purposes:\n  beamwidthFor()  (:60) reads beam_deg          -> recorded provenance\n  _txIsRotator()  (:330) reads getRotatorAddress() -> the behavioural gate\nSo "is this radio directional?" and "can this radio be steered?" are answered by\ndifferent fields. A fixed directional antenna (beam 35, no rotator) is a real\nconfiguration that makes them disagree by design.\n\nPeter\'s rule — "if the radio is defined as omnidirectional i.e beam width 360\ndegrees then no further discovery is needed" — makes beam_deg the truth and\nsteerability a separate question about the rotator.\n\n── three-vocabularies-for-which-radio ── CONFIRMED\n\nThere are three unrelated ways to name a radio, none of which is the SSOT:\n  dash-mode.js:126-127  RX_ROLES/TX_ROLES = rotator|non-rotator|primary|all|rx\n  traceroute.js:213     auto|yagi|omni|<explicit MAC or node_id>\n  channels.js:33        ROLES = pub|pri  (channels only, no radio identity)\n\ntraceroute also carries its own identity translation, toMac() (:51-55), calling\ngetLiveMacByNodeId from ws-relay — MAC/node_id conversion inside a dispatcher,\nwhich is what docs/IDENTITY.md exists to centralise.'}
