2026-08-18 00:29:34,009 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'PETER, 2026-08-18, verbatim:\n\n  "when traceroute is called it has min 3 args, 1 radio, 2 node, 3 timeout. it\n   then resolves the radio, it then looks up the last known info about that node\n   to find out if it was direct or relayed. if it is directional <> 360 degrees\n   then it determines the rotator and aims the antenna via aim() EITHER at the\n   node or it\'s relay node. aim accepts min 2 args bearing and hold time (ms ???)\n   which is the same as the timeout passed to traceroute. once the rotator has\n   confirmed it is aimed it starts the traceroute and saves it. of course some of\n   this is skipped if the radio is an omni directional."\n\n  "aim() resolve() and traceroute() need to be changed to behave like this."\n\n  "implement with /idiot when i wake I expect this to be done and tested. you\n   should be able to call this via post but no dashboard automation will be\n   added for this yet."\n\nSpec: docs/modules/traceroute-flow.md (attached).\n\nTHREE BEHAVIOURS THIS CHANGES, each raised with Peter BEFORE he gave the\ninstruction, so each is his decision and not an assumption:\n\n  1. hold = timeout. 15 s -> 60/90 s on a rotator shared with the garage alarm.\n     rotator.point()\'s own HOLD_CEILING_MS is the independent backstop.\n  2. an unconfirmed aim ABORTS the dispatch, reversing "a rotator that will not\n     move must not stop a trace".\n  3. aim target reduces to node-or-relay, retiring the bypass rule (155 cases)\n     and the do-not-rotate rule.\n\nHe also confirmed the channel question: traceroute is not channel dependent.\nmesh-gw\'s send_traceroute(to) takes only `to`.'}
2026-08-18 00:29:48,664 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'plan', 'text': "DONE AND COMMITTED: 69de050.\n\n  run(radio, node, timeoutMs, opts)   arity 3\n  aimAt(bearing, holdMs, opts)        arity 2\n  aimForNode(to, radio, holdMs)       the composition traceroute calls\n  POST /traceroute { radio, node, timeout_ms }\n  GET  /traceroute_history            restored (a dispatch you cannot read back\n                                      is not testable)\n\nTHE POST PATH HAS NO NODE ID IN IT, DELIBERATELY. The old POST /:nodeId/traceroute\nwas swallowed by index.js's catch-all /^\\/![0-9a-f]+/i proxy once its handler was\ndeleted, so it kept transmitting via mesh-gw with no guards and no row. A\nbody-carrying /traceroute cannot match that regex. Mounted at index.js:361, ahead\nof the proxy at :390.\n\ndash-mode is no longer imported by traceroute.js at all — modeAim() and\ntransmitterForMode() were how a MODE controlled the pointer. That closes\n`mode-must-not-control-aim` at the source.\n\nFOUND IN MY OWN NEW CODE, by the tests: aimAt guarded the bearing with\nNumber.isFinite() alone, and Number(null) === 0 which is DUE NORTH — a null\nbearing pointed the antenna north instead of refusing. Same trap as channel 0 /\nPRIMARY. Fixed before commit.\n\nVERIFIED\n  test_aim 31, test_traceroute_flow 17, test_resolve 31 — all in check_all.\n  Fast suite 10 of 10. All specs current.\n  node-dash restarted, online, rotator connected v4.\n  POST validation exercised live: five bad bodies -> five 400s with reasons.\n\nNOT VERIFIED — needs Peter awake:\n  NOTHING WAS TRANSMITTED AND NOTHING MOVED. Every test stubs bridge.post and\n  rotator._pointOnce, so the path runs in full and stops at the wire and the\n  motor. The 200 path is UNPROVEN ON AIR. One supervised call to\n  POST /traceroute with a real node and a 30-60 s timeout proves it — and that\n  call will move the YAGI and put a frame on air on a channel we do not choose.\n\nCLEANED UP AFTER MYSELF: an early test run was killed mid-flight and left 5\nfabricated rows in traceroute_history (37426-37430). Deleted; the table is back\nto 37,424 real rows, newest 2026-08-17 22:57:06. The test now takes its own\nbaseline and deletes what it creates."}
