2026-08-18 08:22:11,654 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'PETER, 2026-08-18: "this always happens. I ask a question and then are shocked by\nthe answer, reading between the lines we have no SSOT, we have cached vs real\ntime last_heard, we have no record of last trace. that last one I challenge,\nbecause every traceroute saves to and / or updates tables."\n\nHE WAS RIGHT TO CHALLENGE. Last trace IS recorded, in two places that agree. What\nit is not is EXPOSED — it is absent from the node payload, so any consumer has to\nquery traceroute_history for it. I implied it was missing by talking only about\nthe `nodes` columns.\n\nFIVE ANSWERS TO "WHEN DID WE LAST ...", measured on T4 / Preston~Bowyer\n(47500548) on 2026-08-18 at 08:12:\n\n  nodes.last_heard           2026-08-16 14:55   41 h ago    the RADIO\'s nodedb, SECOND-HAND\n  nodes.rf_last_heard        2026-08-18 08:11   1 MIN ago   we heard it on RF, FIRST-HAND\n  last trace ATTEMPT         2026-08-18 08:12   just now    traceroute_history, any status\n  last trace HIT             2026-08-14 14:55   4 days      traceroute_history, status=\'ok\'\n  nodeinfo.last_traceroute   2026-08-14 14:55   4 days      the stored route blob\n\nTHE THREE PARTS\n\n1. NO SSOT. Five timestamps, no single owner, and no documented rule for which\n   question each answers.\n\n2. DECISIONS ARE BOUND TO THE WEAKEST ONE. `last_heard` arrives on\n   node_info/node_update carrying the RADIO\'s cached value — node-list.js:129\n   already says so: "carries the RADIO\'s own cached last_heard, which is stale\n   for any node it hasn\'t personally re-heard recently", with a guard to stop it\n   rolling a fresher entry backwards. Yet:\n     filters.js:84    node sort defaults to last_heard\n     filters.js:91    max-age filter is `last_heard >= unixepoch() - ?`\n     ws-relay.js:481  heard_tone — the node list\'s freshness colour — uses last_heard\n   So for T4 the dashboard paints it 41 hours stale and filters it out of a\n   "heard recently" view ONE MINUTE after we received a packet from it.\n   Same class as the "verified" hop badge: a second-hand number wearing a\n   first-hand name.\n\n3. THE NAME IS MISLEADING and Peter said so first: "if that means last_trace_hit\n   then it is a very poor and confusing key name? to me last_heard means exactly\n   that, last_trace_hit is also explicit." It does NOT mean last trace — but it\n   also does not mean what it says. `nodedb_last_heard` (a report) vs\n   `rf_last_heard` (an observation) would be honest.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS BEYOND TIDINESS — the diagnosis it handed us. We heard T4 sixty\nseconds before it failed to answer a traceroute, and it has not answered one in\nfour days. Five traceroutes on 2026-08-18 timed out across BOTH radios, including\nthe best target we have (T4: 113 attempts, 79 answered, 70% historic). Inbound is\nhealthy; the round trip is not. So the collapse is very unlikely to be about\naiming or the YAGI — it is the return path, and `rf_last_heard` is the column\nthat reveals it while `last_heard` hides it.\n\nPARKED BY PETER: "once we are done with traceroute we will address it." Do not\nstart this while traceroute work is in flight. A column rename touches storage,\nfilters, the node payload and the browser, so it is its own task with its own\nspec.\n\nRELATED, ALREADY FILED: the return-path question itself (why nothing answers) is\nnot this task — this task is about knowing WHICH timestamp to trust.'}
