2026-08-18 09:29:10,983 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'PETER, 2026-08-18, VERBATIM:\n\n"I cannot sit in front of the keyoard today, so I need you to que some tasks, I will be /afk. after you have degbugged, fixed and test this the next thing is the Config -> Device -> Radio settings. all of the code that handles the saving of radio settings including Channels and User and EVERY OTHER radio config needs to be ripped out. The browser i a presentation layer. you need to investigate how the official meshtastic phone app does this an replicate it. This has =been fragile and intermittently working and not working ever since it was written. so 1) Rip out all of the CRUD / code from the backend that loads and saves configs to the RADIO. also I know for 100% sure that the RADIO does NOT need to be rebooted on every config change. We have also discovered that Primary Channel can not be named, and it\'s PSK cannot be changed. you need a new task for this and work it end to end. I do NOT want you to patch the existing code, I want it ripped out and re-done based on the exact same method that the phone app does. a new task is needed for this and you need to work on it autonomusly, I will check what is happening from time to time, but I will not be sat here. /afk"\n\nMY READING, kept separate from his words on purpose:\n\n1. SCOPE = every radio config write path, not just channels. Channels, User/owner, LoRa, device, position, MQTT — "EVERY OTHER radio config". Backend load AND save.\n2. RIP OUT, DO NOT PATCH. Explicit and repeated: "I do NOT want you to patch the existing code, I want it ripped out and re-done". A patch to the existing flow is a failed task even if it works.\n3. THE METHOD IS NOT MINE TO INVENT. "based on the exact same method that the phone app does" — the official Meshtastic app is the reference implementation. Investigate it FIRST; do not design a scheme and then justify it.\n4. NO REBOOT PER CHANGE. He is "100% sure" the radio does not need rebooting on every config change. Treat as a requirement to verify against the app/firmware, not to take on faith — but the current reboot-every-time behaviour is presumed wrong.\n5. PRIMARY CHANNEL: "can not be named, and it\'s PSK cannot be changed." Read as a RULE for our system, discovered the hard way today: a named primary is a channel nobody else is on, and the primary PSK is the published default AQ==. VERIFY whether the phone app actually forbids/greys these, or merely warns — the wording "cannot" needs checking against the app before it becomes a validation rule. See docs/modules/channels.md and commit 4a9b90b for why this matters.\n6. BROWSER IS PRESENTATION ONLY. It renders the form and submits it; it decides nothing, derives nothing, and holds no radio-config logic.\n7. AUTONOMOUS. He is /afk and will look in occasionally. Decide, do, record; do not block on questions.\n\nPRIOR CONTEXT THAT BEARS ON THIS: today\'s outage was caused by a misnamed primary channel that the Config -> Device -> Radio surface is exactly the tool people use to set. Whatever replaces it must make naming a primary either impossible or loudly refused.'}
2026-08-18 09:31:31,965 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'plan', 'text': 'TWO CONSTRAINTS ADDED BY PETER AFTER THE TASK WAS CREATED, 2026-08-18, VERBATIM:\n\n"do not start this until the current task is fixed and tested. /afk"\n"do NOT write any new code until we have established how phone app does it. /afk"\n"and RIP OUT THE EXISTING CODE, DO NOT PATCH IT. /afk"\n\nHe has now said rip-out-not-patch THREE times (once in the original instruction, twice since). A restatement means it has not landed, so it is recorded here as an ACCEPTANCE CRITERION rather than as advice:\n\n  A change that modifies the existing radio-config save/load code is a FAILED\n  task, even if the result works and even if the tests pass. The existing code\n  is deleted first. Nothing is kept "because it already handles X".\n\nORDER OF WORK, forced by the above and not negotiable:\n  1. Current task (traceroute + channel outage) fixed AND tested. Do not begin\n     this task before that is closed out.\n  2. RESEARCH ONLY: establish how the official Meshtastic phone app writes radio\n     config. NO NEW CODE until that is established and written down here.\n  3. Only then: rip out. Then rebuild to the app\'s method.\n\nWHAT "ESTABLISHED" HAS TO MEAN, so step 2 is not declared done on a hunch: the\nadmin/config write protocol the app actually uses, named concretely — the message\ntypes, the session/commit semantics if any, which changes require a reboot and\nwhich do not, and what the app does about the primary channel\'s name and PSK.\nSourced from the app\'s own source or the protobuf definitions, not from a blog\npost and not from my own recollection. Quote it, cite where it came from.\n\nOPEN QUESTION FOR THAT RESEARCH, flagged now: node-dash does not talk to the\nradio directly — mesh-gw does. If mesh-gw does not expose the primitives the\napp\'s method needs, that is an xsession item for mesh-gw and NOT something to\nwork around in node-dash. A workaround makes their gap permanent and invisible.'}
2026-08-18 09:34:12,227 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'note', 'text': 'RESEARCH FINDINGS — step 2, sourced and quoted. NO CODE WRITTEN.\n\nSOURCE A — meshtastic/protobufs/meshtastic/admin.proto (fetched 2026-08-18, 972 lines).\n\n  "Begins an edit transaction for config, module config, owner, and channel settings changes\n   This will delay the standard *implicit* save to the file system and subsequent reboot\n   behavior until committed (commit_edit_settings)"      bool begin_edit_settings = 64;\n  "Commits an open transaction for any edits made to config, module config, owner, and\n   channel settings"                                     bool commit_edit_settings = 65;\n\n  Setters: set_owner = 32, set_channel = 33, set_config = 34, set_module_config = 35.\n  Reboot is a SEPARATE explicit command: int32 reboot_seconds = 97.\n\n  "The node generates this key and sends it with any get_x_response packets. The client\n   MUST include the same key with any set_x commands. Key expires after 300 seconds.\n   Prevents replay attacks for admin messages."          bytes session_passkey = 101;\n\n  => The protocol\'s own answer to Peter: config writes do NOT each need a reboot. The\n     transaction exists specifically to collapse the implicit save+reboot into ONE at commit.\n\nSOURCE B — mesh-gw\'s own docs (docs/gw, read-only reference).\n\n  modules/methods.md:62 — "restore_config uses individual writes with _wait_ready() between\n  each — no begin_edit_settings (firmware ignores the edit lock and reboots immediately\n  after first SET)"\n\n  *** DIRECT CONTRADICTION of Source A, and it decides the whole design. Either the\n  firmware honours the transaction (Source A) or it ignores it (Source B). Unresolved. ***\n\n  API_REST.md "Config write semantics" — all PUT /{node}/config/{section} and\n  PUT /{node}/channels/{index} writes:\n    - "Are fire-and-forget to the radio (Meshtastic firmware never sends a reply)"\n    - "Return {"verified": true} immediately after the BLE write completes"\n    - "Update the in-memory cache so subsequent GETs return fresh values without a round-trip"\n    - "Some sections trigger a device reboot (documented in GET /sections metadata as __reboot: true)"\n\n  => TWO CONSEQUENCES, and they explain "fragile and intermittently working" without\n     needing any node-dash defect at all:\n     1. `verified: true` is not verification. Nothing confirms the radio applied anything.\n     2. node-dash\'s read-back validation (op-manager match_fields) GETs a value that\n        mesh-gw has already optimistically overwritten with what we just sent. It compares\n        our own input against our own input. It cannot fail, so it has never once detected\n        a failed write. Related prior note: "the mesh-gw cache that undermines the\n        diagnosis" (handover 8b26bd3).\n\n  session_passkey IS handled by mesh-gw (app_router extracts it from ADMIN_APP responses ->\n  BleDevice.set_session_passkey; config.admin_passkey_refresh_s; _passkey_refresh_loop), so\n  the 300s expiry is not node-dash\'s to manage — but a refresh that lags a write would\n  produce exactly the intermittency reported, and that is worth asking about.\n\nWHERE THIS LEAVES THE TASK. The rip-out of node-dash\'s radio-config CRUD stands. But the\nREPLACEMENT depends on primitives that live in mesh-gw and on a contradiction only mesh-gw\ncan settle. Per CLAUDE.md and /xsession this is raised with the peer and NOT worked around —\na workaround here would make their defect permanent and invisible, and would also bake in a\nvalidation that cannot fail.\n\nSTILL OPEN, not yet researched: what the phone app does about the PRIMARY channel\'s name and\nPSK (Peter: "can not be named, and it\'s PSK cannot be changed"). Needs checking against the\napp\'s own source before it becomes a validation rule.'}
2026-08-18 09:35:43,219 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'note', 'text': 'OPEN QUESTION FOR THE REWRITE — does renaming the PRIMARY change the frequency slot?\n\nmeshtastic.org/docs/configuration/radio/channels/ states: "a hash of the PRIMARY channel\'s\nname sets the LoRa frequency slot, which determines the actual frequency you are\ntransmitting on in the band". Both our radios run channel_num = 0, which means "derive the\nslot from the primary channel name hash".\n\nMEASURED TODAY, AND IT DOES NOT AGREE WITH THAT. While both radios had their primary named\n`Public` we still RECEIVED third-party packets — 34 nodes updating rf_last_heard, and G1\'s\npackets arriving with readable headers — we simply could not DECRYPT any of them. If the\nname had moved us to a different frequency slot we would have heard nothing at all, not\nundecryptable traffic.\n\nSo on firmware 2.8.0.793a749 the primary\'s name appears to affect the CHANNEL HASH (hence\ndecryption) but NOT the frequency slot. Either the doc is stale, or `Public` and `LongFast`\ncollide in the slot hash, or the slot comes from the modem preset in this version.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS HERE: it changes what the rewrite must protect against. If naming the primary\nonly breaks decryption, the damage is silent-but-recoverable by renaming. If it can also\nmove the frequency, a bad save could put a remote radio somewhere we cannot reach it at all\n— which would make the write path safety-critical rather than merely fragile.\n\nNOT RESOLVED. Needs firmware RadioInterface.cpp read, not another doc. Do not encode either\nassumption in the rewrite until it is settled.'}
2026-08-18 09:36:43,880 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'note', 'text': 'RESOLVED — the frequency-slot question above. The primary\'s NAME cannot move our frequency in EU_868, and the measurement was right.\n\nFIRMWARE, src/mesh/RadioInterface.cpp (fetched 2026-08-18):\n\n  uint32_t numChannels = floor((myRegion->freqEnd - myRegion->freqStart) / (myRegion->spacing + (bw / 1000)));\n  const char *channelName = channels.getName(channels.getPrimaryIndex());\n  uint32_t channel_num = (loraConfig.channel_num ? loraConfig.channel_num - 1 : hash(channelName)) % numChannels;\n\n  Region table: RDEF(EU_868, 869.4f, 869.65f, 10, 0, 27, false, false, false)\n    => freqStart 869.4, freqEnd 869.65, spacing 0\n\n  EU_868 at LongFast (bw 250 kHz):\n    numChannels = floor((869.65 - 869.4) / (0 + 0.25)) = 1\n    hash(name) % 1 == 0  FOR EVERY NAME\n\nSo with channel_num = 0 the slot is always 0 here no matter what the primary is called. The\npublished doc ("a hash of the PRIMARY channel\'s name sets the LoRa frequency slot") is\ncorrect in general and irrelevant in our region.\n\nTHIS MATCHES WHAT WE MEASURED and closes the discrepancy: renaming the primary changed the\nCHANNEL HASH (so decryption failed) but not the frequency (so we still heard everything).\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THE REWRITE — the write path is fragile, not safety-critical, IN THIS REGION.\nA bad primary name silently kills decryption while leaving the radio on the same frequency\nand therefore still reachable and still fixable over the air.\n\nBUT DO NOT GENERALISE IT. This is a property of EU_868 + 250 kHz, not of Meshtastic. In a\nregion with many slots — US 902-928 gives dozens — the same mistake WOULD move the radio\'s\nfrequency and could put a remote unit somewhere we cannot reach. If the rewrite ever writes\nconfig to a radio outside EU_868, naming the primary becomes capable of stranding it.\nEncode the guard on that basis, not on ours being safe.'}
