2026-08-18 10:00:28,147 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'radio-config-rewrite-phone-app-method', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'MESH-GW ANSWERED IN FULL, 2026-08-18 08:43 (xsession #23, my reply #26). This changes the\ntask\'s foundations, and two of its four blockers are now measurements they are running.\n\nCONFIRMED AS THEIR DEFECT, with file:line — my read of "fragile and intermittent" was right\nand it is NOT node-dash\'s fault:\n  core/methods.py:266-278  _write_direct returns {"verified": True} the moment the BLE\n                           write_gatt_char call returns. Nothing else is checked.\n  core/methods.py:318-322  set_config then OVERWRITES the cache with the values it just sent.\n  core/methods.py:153-164  get_config_live is a PURE CACHE READ. Never touches the radio.\n  ble_device.py:866-894    every send_admin goes out want_response=False. There is NO admin\n                           request-response read path in that repo at all.\n  Their words: "your save-then-read-back compares your input against your input and cannot\n  fail. Two weeks of never detecting a failed write is the expected behaviour of this code,\n  not bad luck."\n\nTHE TRANSACTION CONTRADICTION IS RESOLVED — AND THE NOTE WAS WRONG, NOT THE PROTO.\n  eed13b1 (28 Jun) implemented begin/commit; 2531ffb reverted it the SAME DAY, and their\n  methods.py note is that revert. But it was tested on an ESP32-C3/NimBLE device, in June,\n  on an unrecorded firmware generation — never on nRF52, never on 2.8.0.793a749. They say\n  explicitly: "Do not design the rewrite against the current note." They are retesting.\n  Also: restore_config was the ONLY user of the transaction; a single-section PUT never went\n  near begin_edit_settings.\n\nNEW AND POSSIBLY THE ACTUAL CAUSE — session_passkey_ttl_s is 0 on BOTH radios while READY\n  and connected. It should never read 0 when connected (they refresh every 240s against a\n  300s expiry, ble_device.py:1270-1275). If firmware silently discards admin SETs bearing an\n  expired passkey, writes fail intermittently AND silently, and the verified:true defect\n  guarantees nobody notices. I have asked them to prioritise this measurement OVER the\n  transaction one: the transaction changes how we write, the passkey decides whether writes\n  land at all.\n\nI WAS WRONG ABOUT THE PARTIAL READ, and it is worth recording because it removes a whole\ndesign requirement. The three-field GET was NOT a truncation: absent == protobuf default\n(MessageToDict without always_print_fields_with_no_presence, and the wire omits defaults).\nRound-tripping rebuilds a byte-identical protobuf. AND set_config already does\nread-modify-write for us — methods.py:308 _merge_config(cached, submitted), with null\nmeaning "keep cached". So the rewrite does NOT need to solve read-modify-write.\n\nA TRAP FOR THE REWRITE — /schema/{section} EXISTS and is the "never guess" endpoint, but it\nis generated from the DESCRIPTOR IN THEIR INSTALLED PYTHON PACKAGE (meshtastic 2.7.9), not\nfrom the connected firmware (2.8.0.793a749). So it offers values the radio may refuse —\nROUTER_CLIENT and REPEATER among them — and that is why the role write "succeeded" and did\nnot stick. DO NOT build the new form\'s enums from /schema without recording this skew.\n\nDECISIONS I MADE ON NODE-DASH\'S BEHALF (reply #26), all reversible:\n  - fresh-read API shape: ?fresh=1 on the existing GET, not a new method\n  - order: (b) real round-trip read FIRST, then (c) stop updating cache on write, then\n    (a) rename verified -> sent\n  - unnameable role ordinals: ALWAYS A STRING, never a sometimes-object\n\nSTILL BLOCKING, and both are theirs: the transaction measurement on 2.8.0.793a749, and the\npasskey TTL measurement. No node-dash code has been written against any of this.'}
2026-08-18 10:01:06,651 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'last-heard-is-king', 'title': 'BUG - last_heard is KING and must never be stale; the stored column is second-hand and a traceroute reply may not reach it', 'steps': ['Investigate: what writes nodes.last_heard, and can a traceroute reply fail to reach it', 'Document: last_heard is first-hand and monotonic', 'Implement', 'Observe: a traceroute reply moves last_heard, and it never goes backwards', 'Track']}
2026-08-18 10:01:18,062 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'PETER, 2026-08-18, VERBATIM:\n\n"last_heard is king. traceroute is tx/rx so last_heard will also reflect traceroutes. last_heard is KING, it must never be stale"\n\nMY READING, kept separate from his words:\n\n1. last_heard IS THE AUTHORITATIVE FIELD. Not rf_last_heard, not a new heard_at, not a\n   coalesce of two columns. He is naming the field that already exists and saying it is the\n   one that must be right. So the fix is to make last_heard correct, NOT to introduce a\n   better-named sibling and leave last_heard wrong. That kills the "rename it to\n   nodedb_last_heard and bind decisions to rf_last_heard" design I had sketched.\n\n2. A TRACEROUTE REPLY IS A RECEPTION AND MUST COUNT. "traceroute is tx/rx" — when a node\n   answers a traceroute, we have just heard it, so last_heard must move. He is telling me the\n   two are not separate facts.\n\n3. "MUST NEVER BE STALE" is the acceptance criterion. Not "should usually be fresh". If we\n   have heard a node, last_heard says so, immediately.\n\nWHY THIS OVERRIDES WHAT I CONCLUDED EARLIER TODAY. In park-node-recency-no-ssot (closed,\nc5fde49) I found nodes.last_heard holds the RADIO\'s second-hand nodedb claim — T4 read 42.8 h\nstale while we had heard it 30 min earlier — and I classified it as LATENT and not worth\nfixing, because the LIVE payload reads a first-hand in-memory value instead. Peter has just\ntold me that is not acceptable. The stored column wearing the king\'s name while carrying a\nstale second-hand number is the defect, not a curiosity.\n\nTO VERIFY BEFORE BUILDING, because I have already been wrong once today by reasoning from the\nwrong layer:\n - what actually writes nodes.last_heard, and whether ANY first-hand reception reaches it\n - whether a traceroute reply reaches it, or only the generic packet path\n - the scan guard in bridge-events.js:61 (`yagiOnly`) — during a scan, only the scan listener\n   may touch last-heard, so a reply heard by the other radio is DROPPED. That is a concrete\n   way last_heard goes stale while we are actively hearing the node.\n - whether it can ever go BACKWARDS (node-list.js:129 has a guard for the cache; the column\n   may not)'}
2026-08-18 10:02:40,924 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_file_attach args={'file_path': 'docs/modules/last-heard-is-king.md', 'kind': 'plan', 'label': 'Spec — a reception persists, and last_heard only moves forward'}
2026-08-18 10:25:06,118 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'radio-config-rewrite-phone-app-method', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'PETER, 2026-08-18, VERBATIM:\n\n"remember that mesh-gw is a passthrough, it is effectively the BLE layer of phone app. do not ask it to do things it is not responsible for, it does not filter, but the session key is something it should handle"\n\nMY READING, and it changes the shape of this task:\n\n1. MESH-GW IS THE BLE TRANSPORT, NOT THE APPLICATION. It is the phone app\'s BLE layer and\n   nothing above it. So the edit TRANSACTION, the field validation, the ordering, the\n   decision about what to write and in what sequence — all of that is NODE-DASH\'s, because\n   in the phone app it belongs to the app, not to the BLE stack.\n\n2. THE SESSION KEY IS THEIRS. Explicitly carved out. They already inject it\n   (ble_device.py:882-883) and refresh it (:1270-1275), and session_passkey_ttl_s reading 0\n   on both radios while READY stays their bug to fix. I do not touch it and I do not work\n   around it.\n\n3. DO NOT ASK THEM FOR APPLICATION FEATURES. My earlier asks need re-reading against this.\n   Reviewed:\n     (a) rename verified -> sent      OK — honesty about what the transport did\n     (c) stop updating cache on write OK — a passthrough should not invent state\n     (b) "?fresh=1 on GET"            WRONGLY SHAPED. That is a convenience feature I asked\n                                      the transport to build for me. The passthrough ask is\n                                      simply: let me send an AdminMessage and get the reply.\n\nTHE ACTUAL BLOCKER, NOW CORRECTLY IDENTIFIED — mesh-gw does not expose the admin passthrough\nover REST.\n\n  core/ble_device.py  async send_admin(message: dict, to, want_response: bool = False) -> None\n  It EXISTS, and want_response is already in the signature. It is simply never exposed.\n\n  Every documented REST route (API_REST.md), and there is no admin among them:\n    GET /devices, /status, /sections, /{node_id}/messages, /{node_id}/outbox, /ble/scan,\n    /ble/{address}/diagnose, /docs, /openapi.json\n    POST /devices, /devices/{addr}/connect, /ble/{address}/pair, /{node_id}/messages, /ota…\n    PUT  /{node_id}/config/{section}, /{node_id}/channels/{index}\n    DELETE /devices/{addr}, /ble/known/{address}\n\n  So today node-dash CANNOT compose an AdminMessage. The only config write surface is the\n  cooked PUT, which is fire-and-forget, reports verified:true without verifying, and\n  overwrites the cache with what we sent.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THE REWRITE — this is the design, and it follows from Peter\'s sentence:\n\n  node-dash composes the AdminMessage sequence, exactly as the phone app does:\n      begin_edit_settings -> set_config / set_channel / set_owner / set_module_config\n      -> commit_edit_settings\n  mesh-gw carries each one, attaches the session passkey, and returns the reply when\n  want_response is set.\n\n  That needs ONE thing from them that they do not have: an admin passthrough route. It is\n  not an application feature — it is the BLE layer\'s whole job, and the method is already\n  written.\n\n  It also means the rewrite does NOT depend on their transaction measurement in the way I\n  thought: if we drive begin/commit ourselves through the passthrough, we can measure the\n  reboot behaviour on 2.8.0.793a749 OURSELVES, on our own radios. Their measurement becomes\n  a useful cross-check rather than a blocker.\n\nSTILL THEIRS, unchanged: the session_passkey TTL reading 0. I tried to measure it myself and\ncould not — it is not on /status or /devices, and the device_snapshot event I tapped carried\nneither the field nor a node_id. Peter has now confirmed the session key is mesh-gw\'s\nresponsibility, so I am not pursuing it further from this side.'}
