2026-08-18 10:28:51,811 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'bugs', 'kind': 'note', 'text': '[check-specs-has-no-pure-doc-mode] CONFIRMED — scripts/check_specs.py vs CLAUDE.md\n\nCLAUDE.md\'s /idiot config states:\n\n  pure_doc_rule: for files under docs/ with no backing source file, set source_hash to the\n  SHA-256 of the doc file itself\n\nscripts/check_specs.py does not implement it. Lines 63-64:\n\n  source_field = fm.get("source")\n  if not source_field:\n      return "error", module, "frontmatter missing \'source\'"\n\nA spec with no `source:` is a hard ERROR, so the documented mode cannot be used. Verified\n2026-08-18 by writing exactly such a spec: `ERROR tidy-stray-tmp-in-src  frontmatter missing\n\'source\'`.\n\nAND THE RULE AS WRITTEN IS SELF-REFERENTIAL ANYWAY. "the SHA-256 of the doc file itself"\ncannot be satisfied: writing the hash into the frontmatter changes the file, so the stored\nhash never matches the file that contains it. Any implementation has to hash the file with\nthe source_hash line excluded or normalised, and the rule does not say that.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS: it blocks a legitimate and recurring case — a spec describing a DELETION,\nwhose subject no longer exists to be hashed. Hit while removing a committed temp file\n(9364596); the workaround was to put the reasoning in the commit message instead, which is\nweaker because a commit is not indexed with the module docs.\n\nNEEDS A DECISION, not just a fix: either\n  (a) implement the mode — allow `source:` to be absent and hash the doc with its own\n      source_hash line normalised out, or\n  (b) drop pure_doc_rule from CLAUDE.md and say plainly that every spec must name a live\n      source file, so deletions are recorded in an existing module\'s spec or in the commit.\nDo NOT leave CLAUDE.md describing a mode the tool rejects — that is a rule that reads as\navailable and fails on use.'}
2026-08-18 10:30:17,858 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'archive-observatory-and-discovery', 'title': 'TIDY - move observatory and discovery out of the tree into a tarred archive, with their specs', 'steps': ['Investigate: enumerate every file and every live entanglement', 'Document: what is archived, what must stay, and what breaks if it goes', 'Implement: tar into archive/, remove from tree, cut the imports', 'Observe: service boots, suite green, dashboard renders in both themes', 'Track']}
2026-08-18 10:30:31,625 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'PETER, 2026-08-18, VERBATIM:\n\n"really observatory and discovery need moving out of code with the associated specs and put into a archive folder, tarred up so they stop confusing you. /afk"\n\nand earlier the same day: "observator is deprecated."\n\nMY READING:\n\n1. TWO SUBSYSTEMS: observatory AND discovery. Both leave src/ and public/, and their SPECS\n   go with them — "with the associated specs" is explicit. A spec left behind for absent\n   code is exactly the confusion he is trying to end.\n2. ARCHIVE, NOT DELETE. "put into a archive folder, tarred up". The tarball is the point:\n   it stops the content being grep-able and read as live code, while keeping it recoverable.\n   Peter, 2026-08-17, on observatory: "it needs to ve decoupled / disabled NOT deleted. I may\n   re-use parts of it in the future." Archiving honours that; deleting would not.\n3. THE REASON IS MY CONFUSION, and that is a real cost he has watched me pay. Today alone I\n   read observatory files while chasing unrelated faults, and a stray truncated COPY of\n   observatory.js had been sitting tracked in src/ since 02 Aug (removed in 9364596).\n4. TARRED means one archive file, not a directory of loose sources — otherwise the next\n   session greps it and is confused in exactly the same way.\n\nWHAT MUST BE ESTABLISHED BEFORE ANYTHING MOVES, because these are not symmetric:\n\n - OBSERVATORY is already disabled behind observatory.enabled=false (verified: config row\n   reads false). But src/index.js imports it STATICALLY and side-effectingly — its own spec\n   says a registration made later "silently never fires" — so the import has to be cut, not\n   just the flag left off.\n - DISCOVERY is NOT obviously dead. `DISC` is one of the four live dash modes in the UI\n   (PASV/ACTV/SCAN/DISC), `discoveryConfig()` is read by config-api, and DISCOVERY_LIMITS\n   is imported alongside the rotator hold ceiling. Some of that may be load-bearing for\n   things that are NOT discovery.\n\n   So discovery is investigated on its own merits and I do NOT assume it is inert. If part\n   of it is genuinely live, that part stays and the fact is reported — archiving something\n   still in use would be far worse than leaving it.\n\nACCEPTANCE: the service boots, check_all passes, the dashboard renders in both themes, and\n`grep -rn observatory src/ public/` returns nothing that is code.'}
2026-08-18 10:32:02,124 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'plan', 'text': 'PHASE 1 RESULT — observatory archives cleanly, DISCOVERY DOES NOT, and I am not forcing it.\n\nARCHIVING (verified: nothing live depends on any of these)\n  src/observatory.js          index.js imports it, gated at index.js:85 by observatory.enabled\n  src/observations.js         index.js only\n  src/observatory-ws.js       no importer but index-side wiring\n  src/inferences.js           imported unconditionally at index.js:95, "Empty today"\n  src/mission-runner.js       ONLY importer is observatory-ws.js — it goes with observatory\n  public/app-observatory.js, public/partials/tab-observatory.html\n  public/_mockup-mission-control.html\n  tests/test_observatory_boundary.mjs\n  docs/OBSERVATORY_CHARTS.md, docs/OBSERVATORY_REPO_SPEC.md\n  docs/DISCOVERY_STRATEGY.md, docs/DISCOVERY_TARGETING.md\n  docs/modules/{observatory,observatory-ws,observations,inferences,app-observatory,\n                tab-observatory,mission-runner}.md\n\n  index.js:69 states the author\'s own intent: "Delete these lines and the observatory\'s own\n  files and node-dash is unchanged."\n\nSTAYING, and each for a measured reason — archiving these would delete live behaviour Peter\nhas not asked to lose:\n\n  src/relay-observation.js  NOT observatory. It is task `packet-provenance-capture`, imported\n                            by CORE src/persist.js:7. Sounds observatory-shaped, is not.\n  src/doors.js              imported by src/standing-tasks.js, which index.js:484 starts\n                            UNCONDITIONALLY. Live.\n  discoveryConfig()         read by standing-tasks.js in 3 places (max_silence_days) and by\n                            observatory-ws. The standing-tasks uses survive observatory.\n  DISCOVERY_LIMITS          rotator.js:84 uses hold_sec[1] as its hold ceiling — the rotator\n                            is shared with the garage alarm, so this is safety-adjacent.\n  DISC dash mode            dash-mode.js defines it as one of four live modes. Removing a mode\n                            from the UI is a separate decision.\n\nDECISION (/afk): archive observatory + mission-runner + the discovery DOCS, and leave the\nlive discovery machinery alone. "Discovery" is not one thing — the runner is dead, the\nconfig, the door model and the mode are not. Archiving them would break standing-tasks and\nchange the rotator\'s hold ceiling.\n\nWHAT PETER STILL HAS TO DECIDE, and it is NOT safe for me to assume: whether DISC should stop\nbeing an offered dash mode, and whether standing-tasks\' door/discovery analysis is still\nwanted. If both go, doors.js and the discovery config follow and the archive can be\ncompleted. Until then a partial archive is the honest stopping point.\n\nDOMAIN NOTE: this removal touches src/ AND public/ in one task. CLAUDE.md forbids a FIX\nspanning domains; this is an archival removal of a whole subsystem, and halving it leaves the\nbrowser with a tab whose backend is gone. Recorded as a deliberate, reasoned exception rather\nthan an oversight.'}
