2026-08-18 10:55:13,524 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'radio-config-rewrite-phone-app-method', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'PETER, 2026-08-18, VERBATIM:\n\n"mesh-gw role is effectively the BLE layer of phone-app, if things are missing from that such as the passkey then it should fix it. but you cannot expect it do do what layers below the BLE layer in phone app does."\n\nMY READING: the session passkey IS the BLE/admin session and is theirs — they are fixing it. But FIRMWARE behaviour sits BELOW the BLE layer, and asking mesh-gw to measure it is asking the transport to answer for the device. I had queued two such asks and both were misplaced:\n\n  - "measure whether the transaction defers the save+reboot on 2.8.0.793a749"\n  - "measure whether firmware discards an admin SET bearing an expired passkey"\n\nmesh-gw had already said as much in #32: "what the firmware does above the transport is readable from Meshtastic\'s own source, which is as available to you as to me, and me measuring it does not make it more true." Peter has now said it from the other direction. Both right; I read the firmware instead.\n\n=== SETTLED FROM FIRMWARE SOURCE — PETER IS RIGHT, AND IT IS NOT CLOSE ===\n\nsrc/modules/AdminModule.cpp + AdminModule.h, fetched 2026-08-18:\n\n  void AdminModule::saveChanges(int saveWhat, bool shouldReboot)\n  {\n      if (!hasOpenEditTransaction) {\n          LOG_INFO("Save changes to disk");\n          service->reloadConfig(saveWhat);\n      } else {\n          LOG_INFO("Delay save of changes to disk until the open transaction is committed");\n      }\n      if (shouldReboot && !hasOpenEditTransaction) {\n          reboot(DEFAULT_REBOOT_SECONDS);\n      }\n  }\n\n  case begin_edit_settings:  hasOpenEditTransaction = true;\n  case commit_edit_settings: disableBluetooth();\n                             hasOpenEditTransaction = false;\n                             saveChanges(SEGMENT_CONFIG | SEGMENT_MODULECONFIG |\n                                         SEGMENT_DEVICESTATE | SEGMENT_CHANNELS |\n                                         SEGMENT_NODEDATABASE);\n\n  AdminModule.h:44   void saveChanges(int saveWhat, bool shouldReboot = true);\n  AdminModule.cpp:906  if (requiresReboot && !hasOpenEditTransaction) disableBluetooth();\n\nTWO INDEPENDENT REASONS THE RADIO DOES NOT NEED A REBOOT PER CONFIG CHANGE:\n\n1. WITH A TRANSACTION OPEN, NEITHER THE DISK WRITE NOR THE REBOOT HAPPENS. Both are guarded\n   by !hasOpenEditTransaction. N field writes produce ZERO saves and ZERO reboots until\n   commit, and commit does ONE save of all segments and ONE reboot (shouldReboot defaults to\n   true and the flag has just been cleared).\n\n2. MANY CHANGES DO NOT REQUIRE A REBOOT AT ALL, transaction or not. `requiresReboot = false`\n   is set at SIX sites — e.g. when no LoRa radio parameters actually changed (:786, :792) and\n   when security.serial_enabled is unchanged (:899). The firmware already computes whether a\n   given edit needs one.\n\n=== WHICH KILLS MESH-GW\'S NOTE, AND THEY ALREADY CONCEDED IT ===\n\nTheir methods.md said "firmware ignores the edit lock and reboots immediately after first\nSET". The current firmware source says the exact opposite, explicitly and in two places.\nTheir note came from a June test on an ESP32-C3/NimBLE device on an unrecorded firmware\ngeneration, and they had already told me not to design against it. It is now not merely\nuntrusted but contradicted by the authority.\n\n=== CONSEQUENCE: THE METHOD IS FULLY ESTABLISHED, AND NOTHING BLOCKS IT BUT ONE ROUTE ===\n\n  begin_edit_settings\n    -> set_config / set_module_config / set_channel / set_owner  (as many as needed)\n    -> commit_edit_settings          == one save, at most one reboot\n\nnode-dash composes that sequence; mesh-gw carries each AdminMessage and attaches the session\npasskey. That is exactly the phone app\'s division, and it is what Peter asked for.\n\nSTILL NEEDED FROM MESH-GW, and it is the only thing: an admin pass-through route. send_admin\nalready exists (core/ble_device.py) with want_response in its signature; it is simply not\nexposed over REST. Everything else on their list is removal, which makes this cleaner.\n\nNO CODE WRITTEN. Step 2 ("establish the method") is now COMPLETE and sourced.'}
2026-08-18 11:17:28,416 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'radio-config-rewrite-phone-app-method', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'PETER, 2026-08-18, VERBATIM — why he asked for the backup test:\n\n"the reason I asked for that is 2fold. 1 before saving a new config, maybe we should backup the current config. and 2) it can prove that what we are writing to the radio is actually what the radio hold. this might be a permeannt validation mthod or it migh only be a debug / dev tool."\n\nMY READING: (2) is the important one. The read-back IS the replacement for `verified: true`.\nThe reason our save-and-validate has never once detected a failed write is that it read back\nmesh-gw\'s cache, which had just been overwritten with what we sent. A read that goes to the\nRADIO can fail, and a check that can fail is the only kind worth having. (1) is the safety\nnet for a roof-mounted radio.\n\nMEASURED COST, so the permanent-vs-dev-tool question is answered with numbers rather than\ntaste. Against !2687afb1 via POST /{id}/admin get_config_request + ADMIN_APP reply on WS:\n\n  DEVICE_CONFIG    421 ms      LORA_CONFIG      493 ms\n  POSITION_CONFIG  496 ms      POWER_CONFIG     494 ms\n  DISPLAY_CONFIG   449 ms\n  mean 471 ms (n=5)\n  full backup, 10 config + 15 module_config + 8 channels = 33 reads -> ~15.5 s\n\nRECOMMENDATION, both permanent:\n\n  READ-BACK VERIFICATION — PERMANENT, not a dev tool. ~471 ms for the one section you just\n  wrote is nothing next to a save the operator is already waiting on, and it is the only\n  honest basis for the word "saved". Anything less and we are back to asserting an outcome\n  we did not observe, which is the defect this whole task exists to remove.\n\n  BACKUP BEFORE WRITE — PERMANENT and affordable at ~15.5 s. Cheap insurance before mutating\n  a radio on a roof mast where recovery is measured in hours. Reasonable to skip for a\n  single-field edit and always take before a multi-section save or a channel change.\n\nTWO DESIGN CONSTRAINTS THAT FALL OUT OF THE FIRMWARE, and both change WHEN the read-back\nhappens. Not yet verified on air — flagged so they are not assumed:\n\n  a. During an open edit transaction the firmware applies changes but defers the disk write\n     (AdminModule saveChanges, guarded by !hasOpenEditTransaction). A get_config_request\n     mid-transaction most likely reads the PENDING in-RAM value, which would make a\n     read-back inside the transaction prove nothing about what was persisted. So read-back\n     belongs AFTER commit_edit_settings, not between the sets.\n  b. commit ends in reboot(DEFAULT_REBOOT_SECONDS) when requiresReboot. So the read-back\n     must wait for the radio to come back READY, not fire immediately after commit. A\n     read-back that races the reboot will either time out or read the pre-reboot state.\n\n  Both need confirming on air with Peter present — (b) in particular means the verification\n  step has to tolerate a disconnect/reconnect in the middle of it.\n\nTHE BACKUP AS IT EXISTS TODAY IS NOT FIT FOR (2). GET /{id}/radio_backup returned in 2.5 ms\n— a cache dump, not a radio read; mesh-gw\'s own API_REST says GETs are served from cache\n"without a round-trip". Verified 2026-08-18 that it MATCHES the radio on 6 of 6 sections\nchecked (device, lora, channel 0 PRIMARY, channel 2 Private incl. PSK, security, position),\nso it is accurate today — but it would match just as confidently after a FAILED write,\nbecause the cache is updated from what we send. Using it to prove a write landed would be\ncircular.\n\n  => the backup node-dash takes must be assembled from admin get_*_request round trips.\n     That also makes it node-dash\'s job rather than mesh-gw\'s: assembling a backup document\n     is application work, not BLE transport, under the boundary Peter has drawn.'}
2026-08-18 11:18:19,339 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'radio-config-rewrite-phone-app-method', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'PETER, 2026-08-18, VERBATIM:\n\n"by having a yymmddhis filename we have a full record of all changes made to any device, if this is done before EVERY save"\n\nMY READING — this promotes the backup from a safety net to an AUDIT TRAIL, and it changes one\nof my own recommendations.\n\n1. BEFORE EVERY SAVE, not "before risky saves". I had said it was "reasonable to skip for a\n   single-field edit". That is now wrong: skipping any save breaks the chain, and a chain\n   with holes is not a record. At ~15.5 s and ~2.6 KB per backup, there is no cost argument\n   for skipping.\n\n2. THE DIFF IS THE RECORD. Consecutive backups for one device give, for every save ever made:\n   what it was immediately before, and — from the next backup, or from the post-commit\n   read-back — what it became. Nobody has to have written down what they changed. That is a\n   far stronger property than a backup, and it answers the question this project keeps\n   failing to answer: "what changed, and when?" The 16 Aug channel rename would have been a\n   one-line diff instead of two days of inference.\n\n3. IT ALSO DATES THE DAMAGE. A misconfiguration acquires a timestamp and a before-state, so\n   recovery stops being reconstruction and becomes restore-the-previous-file.\n\nONE AMBIGUITY IN THE FORMAT, flagged rather than guessed. `yymmddhis` reads as the PHP date\nformat, where `h` is the 12-HOUR clock and `i` is minutes. Two saves at 09:15:00 and 21:15:00\nwould both render 091500 and collide, and the files would sort wrongly. I am using 24-hour:\n\n    <node_id>/YYMMDDHHMMSS.json      e.g.  !2687afb1/260818104512.json\n\n  - 24-hour so lexical sort == chronological sort, which is what makes the chain readable\n  - node_id first so one device\'s history is one directory listing\n  - seconds included; two saves inside one second is not a real case, and if it ever is the\n    write should fail rather than silently overwrite a record\n\n  If Peter meant 12-hour literally, say so and I will change it — but a record that sorts\n  wrongly and can collide is not a record, so 24-hour is the safe default.\n\nSTORAGE: ~2.6 KB per file measured (the OMNI\'s full backup is 2,623 bytes). 1,000 saves is\n2.6 MB. No retention policy needed, and I am not adding one — silently deleting audit history\nis worse than the disk cost by any measure.\n\nSTILL OPEN: whether the POST-commit read-back is stored as its own file or only used to\nverify. Storing it makes each save a before/after PAIR rather than relying on the next save\nto close the record — which matters because the LAST save before a problem would otherwise\nhave no after-state on disk. Leaning towards storing both, tagged, but that is Peter\'s call\nand it doubles the file count, not the risk.'}
2026-08-18 11:20:02,815 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'radio-config-rewrite-phone-app-method', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'PETER, 2026-08-18, VERBATIM:\n\n"the mesh-gw is supposed to be the only thing that communicates with the radios, so I would trust the cache it has. the only reason I used my phone earlier was because the node-dash is broken"\n\nAGREED ON THE PRINCIPLE, WITH ONE EXCEPTION THAT IS EXACTLY OUR BUG.\n\nSole-writer is the right architecture and his phone use was forced, not habitual. But\nsingle-writer does not by itself make a cache truthful, because mesh-gw updates it from what\nit SENT, not from what the radio CONFIRMED (core/methods.py:318-322). Three ways it can\ndiverge, and only the second involves a second writer:\n\n  1. A WRITE THAT DID NOT LAND. The cache records the intention as a fact. This is not\n     hypothetical and had nothing to do with the phone: the role write on 2026-08-04 returned\n     verified:true, the cache then read ROUTER_CLIENT, and the radio never adopted it — it\n     stayed CLIENT. Cause now known: /schema offered ROUTER_CLIENT from mesh-gw\'s pip\n     protobufs (2.7.9) and the radio (2.8.0) would not take it.\n  2. Another writer — the phone. Acknowledged, and going away once node-dash works.\n  3. The radio changing itself — factory reset, on-device buttons, firmware defaults.\n\nWHAT MAKES HIS POSITION TRUE: mesh-gw\'s own fix (c), which they have already agreed to —\nstop updating the cache on write; update it when the radio re-announces or when a real read\nconfirms. After that the cache IS a mirror of confirmed radio state and deserves exactly the\ntrust he is describing. Before that it is a record of intentions.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THE BACKUP DESIGN — it is a two-stage thing, not a permanent choice:\n\n  NOW, until (c) lands: backups are assembled from admin get_*_request round trips. ~471 ms\n  per section, ~15.5 s for all 33. Slow, and correct.\n\n  AFTER (c): GET /{id}/radio_backup is legitimate and is 2.5 ms — roughly 6,000x faster —\n  because the cache would then only ever hold confirmed radio state. Revisit and switch.\n\n  The READ-BACK after a write stays a genuine radio read either way. Its whole job is to\n  prove the write landed, and proving that against any cache — however well maintained — is\n  circular. That one does not become cache-sourced even after (c).\n\nMEASURED TODAY, and it supports his confidence: the cache-sourced backup matched genuine\nradio reads on 6 of 6 sections checked (device, lora, channel 0 PRIMARY, channel 2 Private\nincluding PSK, security, position). The cache is accurate right now. The objection is not\nthat it is wrong today — it is that nothing in the current design would tell us when it\nstopped being right.'}
2026-08-18 11:26:06,487 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'radio-config-rewrite-phone-app-method', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'SCOPE, FINAL — PETER, 2026-08-18, VERBATIM:\n\n"anyway, it\'s not your responsibility to ensure that mesh-gw is /is not stale"\n\nThis ends a line of design I had been over-building. Consolidating the whole boundary in one\nplace so the next session does not re-derive it:\n\nNODE-DASH\'S JOB\n  - compose the AdminMessage sequence: begin_edit_settings -> set_* -> commit_edit_settings\n  - read config FROM mesh-gw and trust what it returns\n  - take the timestamped backup before every save (the audit chain)\n  - present it; the browser decides nothing\n\nNOT NODE-DASH\'S JOB — and I had drifted into all four\n  - policing whether mesh-gw\'s cache is fresh\n  - building a shadow "genuine read" path to second-guess it\n  - measuring their session_passkey TTL (I tried; Peter stopped it twice)\n  - asking them to measure FIRMWARE behaviour, which is below their layer\n\nIf the cache is wrong, that is a mesh-gw DEFECT — raise it in xsession and let them fix it.\nIt is not a thing node-dash routes around. A workaround makes their defect permanent and\ninvisible, which is the standing rule and I nearly broke it by designing verification\nmachinery whose real purpose was distrust.\n\nWHAT THIS DOES TO THE READ-BACK. I had it as a permanent genuine-radio read on the grounds\nthat verifying a write against a cache is circular. That reasoning holds ONLY while the cache\nis updated from writes. After mesh-gw\'s fix (c) — cache updated from confirmed reads and\nre-announcements, never from sends — a rejected value simply never appears in the cache, so\nreading the cache back DOES reveal the rejection. The parallel path stops being necessary.\n\nSo: no shadow read path. Read back through mesh-gw like everything else, and if that proves\ninsufficient, that is a bug report, not a feature I build here.\n\nWHY THE CACHE IS SOUND, verified from their docs rather than assumed (ble_device.md:21):\n  "Run config/node sync (SYNCING): send want_config_id, drain FROMRADIO in a tight read loop\n   until config_complete"\n  and RECONNECTING -> SYNCING -> READY.\nEvery reconnect rebuilds the cache from the radio. BLE is single-client, so a phone session\nforces a disconnect and therefore a resync. Peter: "if I use my phone to change config, the\nmesh gw needs to be disconnected, when it reconnects it will re-read the configs." Correct.\n\nThe admin pass-through is still needed and still the thing to build against — not to bypass\nthe cache, but because composing the edit transaction is the application\'s job and that is\nthe method the phone app uses.'}
