2026-08-18 11:53:44,727 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'owner-form-role-truth', 'title': 'BUG - the Owner form shows role CLIENT while the radio holds CLIENT_BASE', 'steps': ["Investigate: where the form's role comes from and what we already know", 'Document: serve the role we hold, never a fallback guess', 'Implement', 'Observe: the form shows CLIENT_BASE, and a save still cannot demote the radio', 'Track']}
2026-08-18 11:53:57,463 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'Found while verifying the radio-config rewrite (8363e62), filed in the bug ledger as\n`owner-form-shows-CLIENT-radio-holds-CLIENT_BASE`, now being fixed.\n\nTHE DEFECT. Devices > radio > Owner renders ROLE = "CLIENT". The radio holds CLIENT_BASE.\n\n  GET /!2687afb1/owner  (mesh-gw\'s cooked endpoint, what the form loads)\n      {"short_name":"TA2o","long_name":"TA26 OMNI","__reboot":true,"__notes":[...]}\n      -> role is ABSENT\n  admin get_owner_request, want_response=true (the radio\'s own answer)\n      {... "role":"CLIENT_BASE" ...}\n\nThe form falls back to a hard-coded \'CLIENT\' when role is missing, so the operator is shown\na role the radio does not have.\n\nHARMLESS TODAY ONLY BY COINCIDENCE. public/app-config.js saveOwner does:\n    const currentRole = this.ownerData?.role ?? \'CLIENT\';\n    if (payload.role === currentRole) delete payload.role;\nownerData.role is undefined, so currentRole computes to \'CLIENT\', the displayed value is\nalso \'CLIENT\', they compare equal, and role is stripped. Change either half — a different\ndefault, or a form that sends role unconditionally — and saving the Owner tab DEMOTES the\nradio from CLIENT_BASE to CLIENT.\n\nSame shape as the two failures that cost this week: an operator acting on a screen that does\nnot show what the radio holds.\n\nWE ALREADY KNOW THE ANSWER. No admin round trip is needed:\n    nodes.role     = CLIENT_BASE   for both !2687afb1 and !fa39f7b4\n    nodeinfo.role  = CLIENT_BASE\nBoth are populated from the radio\'s own NodeInfo announcements — first-hand, not a guess and\nnot mesh-gw\'s cooked view. A role change reboots the radio, which re-announces, so it\nself-heals.\n\nDECISION (/afk): serve the role we already hold, from the backend, rather than (a) adding an\nasync admin get_owner_request round trip for one field, or (b) waiting on mesh-gw to stop\ndropping it. Reasons: it does not wait on another repo; it uses our own first-hand data\nrather than second-guessing mesh-gw\'s cache, which Peter has ruled is not my job to police;\nand it is ~0 ms rather than ~471 ms on a form load.\n\nSTILL WORTH TELLING MESH-GW, and I will: a cooked GET that silently drops a field the radio\ndoes answer with is the same information-losing pattern they are already removing\n(verified:true, /schema enums, _FORCED_VALUES). Raising it is not the same as waiting for it.\n\nACCEPTANCE: the form shows CLIENT_BASE for both radios; a save with role untouched still\nsends no role; and the browser gains no fallback of its own — if the backend cannot say what\nthe role is, the form must not invent one.'}
2026-08-18 11:54:21,947 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_file_attach args={'file_path': 'docs/modules/radio-admin.md', 'kind': 'plan', 'label': 'Spec — adds GET /radio-admin/owner/:device, role from our own NodeInfo'}
2026-08-18 11:59:39,176 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'bugs', 'kind': 'note', 'text': '[role-dropdown-offers-deprecated-values] CONFIRMED — Devices > radio > Owner, role field\n\nPeter, 2026-08-18: "CLIENT_BASE - ah yes that\'s another bug, node-dash\'s role section does\nnot match the current firmware."\n\nMEASURED, and the mismatch is narrower than "does not match" — which matters, because the\nnarrow version is the actionable one:\n\n  GET /schema/owner  role field offers 13 values:\n    CLIENT, CLIENT_MUTE, ROUTER, ROUTER_CLIENT, REPEATER, TRACKER, SENSOR, TAK,\n    CLIENT_HIDDEN, LOST_AND_FOUND, TAK_TRACKER, ROUTER_LATE, CLIENT_BASE\n\n  meshtastic/protobufs master, Config.DeviceConfig.Role — the SAME 13 names at the SAME\n  ordinals, but two carry a marker the form does not:\n    ROUTER_CLIENT = 3 [deprecated = true];\n    REPEATER      = 4 [deprecated = true];\n\n  missing from the form: NONE.  offered but not in firmware: NONE.\n\nSo the defect is not a stale value list. It is that the DEPRECATION FLAG IS LOST between the\nprotobuf descriptor and the form, and the operator is offered two dead roles as ordinary\nchoices with nothing to distinguish them.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS. ROUTER_CLIENT is exactly what was written on 2026-08-04, reported\nverified:true, and did not stick. mesh-gw\'s own account: "ROUTER_CLIENT (=3, deprecated but\nstill present in 2.7.9\'s protobufs) parsed cleanly, so we serialised role=3 and reported\nsuccess. We do not validate values against the firmware... Whether the radio dropped the SET\nor took it and coerced it, I cannot tell you, and I am not going to guess."\n\nNOT PROVEN, and I am not asserting it: that the radio REFUSES a deprecated value. Nobody has\nmeasured it. What is proven is that the form offers it, we sent it, and the role did not\nchange.\n\nWHERE THE FLAG IS LOST. mesh-gw generates /schema from the descriptor in ITS installed\nmeshtastic pip package (2.7.9) and does not carry `deprecated` through. The descriptor HAS\nit. This is the same information-losing pattern as the cooked GET /owner dropping `role`\n(fixed in 3dfb96c) and as verified:true — all three are a cooked surface discarding something\nthe authority provides.\n\nTWO FIXES, not equivalent:\n (a) mesh-gw carries `deprecated` through into /schema. Smallest change, but they have said\n     they would rather DELETE or demote /schema than keep patching it: "A transport should\n     not be answering \'what values does this firmware accept\' at all — you should read\n     Meshtastic\'s protobufs, which is the authority for both of us."\n (b) node-dash sources enums from the Meshtastic protobufs directly rather than from\n     mesh-gw\'s /schema. Matches what mesh-gw is asking for and removes the version-skew\n     class entirely — their pip package version stops being able to shape our UI. Bigger:\n     needs the protobufs vendored or fetched, and pinned to a known version.\n\nRECOMMEND (b), and raise (a) with mesh-gw as the interim so the flag is not simply lost\nwhile (b) is built. Peter\'s call on the ordering — (b) is real work and touches how the whole\nConfig surface gets its vocabulary, not just role.'}
2026-08-18 12:00:47,981 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'radio-config-value-validation', 'title': 'BUG - no layer validates radio config values; out-of-range values reach the radio and break it', 'steps': ['Investigate: trace every layer that could validate and prove none does', 'Document: validate in the backend against the schema, refuse before the write', 'Implement', 'Observe: an out-of-range value is refused and never reaches mesh-gw', 'Track']}
2026-08-18 12:01:04,591 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'PETER, 2026-08-18, VERBATIM:\n\n"in fact none of the fields are validated. I can enter any value I like for things like send freq for metadata, node dash allows it and saves it an breaks the radio because the vales sent are invalid"\n\nCONFIRMED, and it directly falsifies a decision I made an hour earlier.\n\nTHE CHAIN, traced end to end. Every layer that could validate, and none does:\n\n  1. mesh-gw\'s /schema DOES carry the constraint. GET /schema/telemetry:\n       {"name":"device_update_interval","type":"int","unit":"seconds","min":1800,\n        "hint":"min 1800 (firmware-enforced); 0 = use default (3600 s)"}\n     Five telemetry intervals all carry min 1800.\n  2. public/app-forms.js buildForm:119-124 sets input.min / input.max as HTML\n     ATTRIBUTES. That is advisory ONLY — the form is read by JS (collectForm),\n     never natively submitted, so the browser\'s constraint validation never runs.\n  3. _collectFromInputs decides only WHETHER to include a field (never read and\n     never touched -> skip). It calls _readFieldValue and does NO range or type\n     check whatsoever.\n  4. src/radio-admin-api.js — MINE, written today — validates presence and shape\n     only (device required, values must be an object). No range check.\n  5. mesh-gw does not validate, and is actively REMOVING what little it had\n     (_FORCED_VALUES, all-keys-required) as transport overreach.\n  6. The radio takes the value. Peter\'s report: it breaks.\n\nMY OWN COMMENT IN radio-admin-api.js WAS THE WRONG CALL AND IS NOW DISPROVED:\n"There is deliberately NO allowlist of section names here... The radio is the\nauthority on what it accepts, and it answers with ADMIN_BAD_SESSION_KEY /\nBAD_REQUEST when it does not." That reasoning holds for VALUES the firmware\nrejects. It does not hold for values the firmware ACCEPTS and is then broken by,\nwhich is what Peter has actually experienced. A device that does not defend itself\ncannot be the last line of defence.\n\nDECISION (/afk): validate in the BACKEND, against the schema, and refuse before\nanything is sent. Not in the browser — the browser decides nothing\n(BROWSER_CONTRACT), and a check that lives only in the form is bypassed by any\nother caller and by a stale page. The backend is the last place the value passes\nthrough that is ours.\n\n  - reject with 400 naming every offending field and the constraint it broke,\n    so the operator is told WHICH value and WHY, not just "failed"\n  - validate type (int/float/bool/enum) and range (min/max) from the same schema\n    the form renders from, so form and backend cannot disagree\n  - refuse the whole save on any violation. A partial write of a multi-field\n    section is worse than no write.\n\nKNOWN LIMITATION, stated rather than hidden: the schema comes from mesh-gw, which\ngenerates it from ITS installed protobuf package (2.7.9), not from the connected\nfirmware (2.8.0). So these constraints are as good as that package and no better —\nthe same skew that offers deprecated ROUTER_CLIENT (ledger:\nrole-dropdown-offers-deprecated-values). It is still a large improvement over\nnothing, and it does not make the skew worse. Sourcing enums and ranges from the\nMeshtastic protobufs directly is the durable fix and is its own task.'}
2026-08-18 12:02:00,812 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'plan', 'text': 'PARKED BY PETER, 2026-08-18, VERBATIM:\n\n"so basicall we need to extract the schema from firmware, id, label, max value, default, options[]."\n"that\'s another bigger job. I would like to see stable and repeatable radio config saves first."\n\nSo this task does NOT proceed to implementation now. Recording one finding first,\nbecause it changes the shape of that bigger job and would otherwise be discovered\nhalfway through it:\n\nTHE PROTOBUF DOES NOT CARRY RANGES. Checked meshtastic/protobufs master,\nmodule_config.proto:\n\n    /*\n     * Interval in seconds of how often we should try to send our\n     * device metrics to the mesh\n     */\n    uint32 device_update_interval = 1;\n\nA type, a field number, and a prose comment. No min, no max, no default beyond\nproto3\'s implicit zero.\n\nSo of the five things Peter listed:\n    id        -> yes, the protobuf field name\n    label     -> derivable from the field name / leading comment\n    options[] -> yes, and BETTER than what we have: the enum carries\n                 [deprecated = true] on ROUTER_CLIENT and REPEATER, which\n                 mesh-gw\'s /schema drops entirely\n    default   -> proto3 zero-values only. The FIRMWARE\'s operational defaults\n                 (e.g. "0 = use default (3600 s)") are not in the proto\n    max value -> NOT PRESENT AT ALL\n\nThe "min 1800 (firmware-enforced)" figure in mesh-gw\'s /schema is hand-written by\nthem from firmware behaviour, not generated. Getting real ranges means parsing\nfirmware C++ (the requiresReboot / clamp logic in AdminModule.cpp and the module\nimplementations), or maintaining a curated table with its own staleness problem.\n\nThat is why it is a bigger job, and Peter is right to sequence it after stable\nsaves. Ranges are the part with no clean source; ids, labels and options are the\neasy three-quarters.\n\nSTILL TRUE AND STILL UNFIXED: nothing in the chain validates anything (see the\ngoal note). Any value the operator types reaches the radio. Peter has chosen to\naccept that for now in favour of getting saves stable first — recorded as HIS\ncall, not as something I judged unimportant.'}
