2026-08-18 13:43:26,883 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'bugs', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'radio-backup-omits-every-module-config-section — CONFIRMED, real\n\nsrc/radio-admin.js backupBeforeSave() writes a snapshot that contains ONLY the 8\nconfig sections. All 13 module_config sections are absent.\n\nMEASURED 2026-08-18 on backups/!2687afb1/260818133755.json, the backup taken\nimmediately before a real ambient_lighting save:\n\n  top-level keys: device, position, power, network, display, lora, bluetooth,\n                  security, sessionkey, device_ui\n  module sections present: NONE\n  missing: ambient_lighting, audio, canned_message, detection_sensor,\n           external_notification, mqtt, neighbor_info, paxcounter, range_test,\n           remote_hardware, serial, store_forward, telemetry\n\nWHY IT MATTERS. The backup exists to be an audit chain. Peter, on why he wanted\nit: "before saving a new config, maybe we should backup the current config", "it\ncan prove that what we are writing to the radio is actually what the radio hold",\nand "by having a yymmddhis filename we have a full record of all changes made to\nany device, if this is done before EVERY save."\n\nIt is not a full record. For 13 of 21 sections it records nothing at all, and a\nrestore from one of these files would silently leave every module config at\nwhatever the radio currently holds. Worse, those 13 are exactly the sections that\n286e70d just made saveable — so the chain is emptiest precisely where changes are\nnow about to start happening.\n\nAlso note `sessionkey` appears in the snapshot. That wants checking against the\nrule that the session passkey is mesh-gw\'s to keep and must never be stored by\nnode-dash — Peter: "that secret is theirs to keep". NOT yet examined; it may be an\nempty placeholder. Do not assume either way.\n\n  where: src/radio-admin.js, backupBeforeSave()\n  next:  find what it reads (probably a single /config GET that only covers\n         Config, not ModuleConfig), and fetch both. Then re-verify by diffing a\n         backup against a live read of all 21 sections.\n\n---\n\nsave-error-does-not-mean-the-write-failed — CONFIRMED, real\n\nPOST /radio-admin/module-config returned\n    Bridge 500: (BleakGATTProtocolErrorCode.UNLIKELY_ERROR: 14,\n                 \'GATT Protocol Error: Unlikely Error\')\nand OMNI dropped to RECONNECTING. THE WRITE HAD ALREADY LANDED — after reconnect\nand re-read, the value was as sent.\n\nObserved twice on 2026-08-18 (146->200 and 200->146), both reported as errors,\nboth persisted.\n\nSo a 502 from this endpoint proves nothing about the radio\'s state. Any "did it\nsave?" logic, any retry, and anything that tells the operator "failed" is\ncurrently capable of being wrong in the dangerous direction — a retry after a\nfalse failure re-sends a write that already applied.\n\n  where: src/radio-admin.js sendAdmin/write, and public/app-config.js asyncOp\n  next:  after any error, read back before reporting. The truthful states are\n         "saved", "not saved", and "unknown — read back to find out", and only\n         the first two should ever be shown as certain.\n\n---\n\nfour-unexplained-writes-to-omni — CONFIRMED they happened, cause UNKNOWN\n\nFour backups exist for !2687afb1 at 11:40:32, 11:40:51, 11:49:52 and 11:56:50 on\n2026-08-18. A backup file is created ONLY by write(), and write() is reached only\nfrom the six setters in src/radio-admin.js — so four writes were sent to a live\nradio and I cannot say by what.\n\nRULED OUT by measurement, not by reasoning:\n  - tests/test_page_snapshot.py — ran it, backup count unchanged\n  - tests/test_playwright.py — ran it, backup count unchanged. Its live channel\n    round-trip is skipped unless PLAYWRIGHT_LIVE_CHANNEL is set, and it is unset\n  - an idle browser on /devices — open 60s, backup count unchanged\n\nNOT HARMFUL, established: excluding the `ts` field the first and last backups are\nbyte-identical, so nothing on the radio actually changed. These were no-op writes.\n\nRecorded as UNKNOWN rather than guessed. Do not close this by assuming it was the\ntests — that was my first hypothesis and measurement refuted it.\n\n  next:  log the caller. radio-admin\'s write() should record what asked for it,\n         because right now a write to a live radio leaves a backup and no\n         attribution.'}
