2026-08-18 15:34:42,854 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'save-follows-the-app-optimistic-then-reread-on-open', 'title': "BUG - synchronous read-back verification cannot work; replace it with the phone app's model", 'steps': ["Investigate: prove the false alarm and read the app's actual model", 'Document: optimistic update, expect the restart, re-read on next open', 'Implement', 'Observe: a save that turns a bool off no longer cries wolf, and reopening re-reads', 'Track']}
2026-08-18 15:35:05,233 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'PETER, 2026-08-18, reporting a real false alarm from the UI:\n\n"store forward did NOT match after saving — is_server: sent false, radio has true"\n\nthen: "seems to me that this will never work"\nthen, when I answered with a menu instead of an answer:\n"how does phone app handle it. that will always be my reply"\n\nHE IS RIGHT ON ALL THREE COUNTS.\n\n1. HIS SAVE WORKED. The alarm was mine, and false. Proven from the backup chain\n   and a fresh read:\n\n     backups/!2687afb1/260818153011.json (taken PRE-save)\n        store_forward = {"enabled": true, "heartbeat": true, "is_server": true}\n     radio now, ?fresh=1\n        store_forward = {"enabled": true, "heartbeat": true}\n\n   is_server is ABSENT because false IS the protobuf default and mesh-gw\'s GET\n   omits defaults. The radio took the write.\n\n2. IT IS STRUCTURAL, NOT A TUNING PROBLEM. Synchronous read-back verification\n   cannot work here, for two independent reasons:\n\n     a) THE READ-BACK RACES THE REBOOT. The radio restarts after a save and\n        mesh-gw\'s cache holds pre-save values for ~16s (measured today:\n        RECONNECTING 14:50:35 -> READY 14:50:51). I did not even use ?fresh=1,\n        and fresh would not help while the device is RECONNECTING.\n\n     b) TURNING ANY BOOLEAN OFF MAKES IT VANISH. "Set to default" and "absent"\n        are the same thing on the wire. So the single commonest edit — switching\n        something off — is the one case that can never be confirmed, and my code\n        turned the stale-cache version of it into a MISMATCH alarm.\n\n   An alarm that fires on the normal path is worse than no alarm. My own spec for\n   b038ccf said exactly that about `unconfirmed`, and I then shipped a `mismatch`\n   that does it.\n\n3. I ASKED HIM TO CHOOSE ("cut back, or revert outright?"). That is the menu\n   behaviour I was corrected for with mesh-gw two hours earlier, repeated with\n   Peter. The answer was never his to give — it is in the app.\n\nWHAT THE APP ACTUALLY DOES. RadioConfigViewModel.setConfig / setModuleConfig:\n\n  a) OPTIMISTIC LOCAL UPDATE from what was SENT:\n         state.copy(moduleConfig = state.moduleConfig.copy(\n             store_forward = config.store_forward ?: state.moduleConfig.store_forward, ...))\n     No read-back. No comparison. The word "verified" appears nowhere.\n\n  b) DECLARES THE RESTART UP FRONT, rather than discovering it as an error:\n         expectRestartIfLocal(config.saveRebootBehavior())\n         private fun expectRestartIfLocal(behavior: RebootBehavior) {\n             if (behavior == RebootBehavior.NEVER) return\n             ...\n             if (dest == myNodeNum) nodeRestartTracker.expectRestart()\n         }\n\n  c) AND THE ONE EXCEPTION GIVES THE RULE — their comment, verbatim:\n         "LoRa is intentionally NOT applied optimistically: the firmware can clamp\n          or region-swap (e.g. EU sibling) a LoRa write and applies it live, so the\n          form must reflect the device\'s actual value. It is re-read from the\n          device when the LoRa screen is next opened."\n\n     So: trust the write; where the FIRMWARE MAY ALTER what you sent, re-read —\n     and re-read WHEN THE SCREEN IS NEXT OPENED, never synchronously. By then the\n     radio has rebooted and resynced, which dissolves both (a) and (b) above.\n\nDECISION: replace the synchronous verification with the app\'s model exactly.\nOptimistic update, expect the restart, invalidate the section so the next open\nre-reads from the device. Delete verifySaved and its test rather than leave dead\ncode with a passing test attached.\n\nSCOPE: public/app-config.js, public/app-forms.js, tests, check_all, and the three\nbutton labels in public/partials/tab-devices.html.'}
2026-08-18 15:37:25,324 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_file_attach args={'file_path': 'docs/modules/app-config.md', 'kind': 'plan', 'label': "Spec — the app's model: optimistic, declare the restart, re-read on next open"}
2026-08-18 15:45:26,685 1035192 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'plan', 'text': 'SHIPPED in caaaa99, replacing b038ccf one commit later. Spec:\ndocs/modules/app-config.md -> "A save follows the phone app: optimistic, then\nre-read on next open".\n\n  public/app-config.js          saveSection / saveOwner / saveFixedPosition now\n                                optimistic; sec.loaded = false marks stale\n  public/app-forms.js           verifySaved + sameValue DELETED, replaced by a\n                                comment saying why they cannot work\n  tests/test_verify_saved.mjs   DELETED\n  scripts/check_all.mjs         verify_saved entry removed, with the reason\n  tab-devices.html              three buttons: "saved — radio is restarting"\n\n`sec.loaded = false` is the whole re-read mechanism. onSectionToggle already\nreturns early while loaded is set, so clearing it makes the next open fetch from\nthe device — the app\'s "re-read when the screen is next opened", with no timer and\nno polling.\n\nVERIFIED WITH PETER\'S EXACT SCENARIO, a boolean turned OFF — the case that\nproduced the false alarm. store_forward heartbeat, on -> off, saved from the UI:\n\n  opErr: null            <- NO false alarm. This is the regression under test.\n  sec.loaded: false      <- next open re-reads\n  sec.data.heartbeat + form checkbox: false   <- optimistic update shows it off\n  network log: GET schema, GET config, POST /radio-admin/module-config — and NO\n               GET after the POST, which is the behavioural change\n  radio ?fresh=1 after reconnect: {"enabled": true}\n               heartbeat ABSENT == false == the save took, and it is the same\n               shape as his is_server case that triggered the alarm\n\nThen restored: {"enabled": true, "heartbeat": true} confirmed on the radio.\n15/15 npm run check, All specs current.\n\nWHAT I GOT WRONG, twice, and the pattern is the same both times.\n\nb038ccf invented a verification mechanism the reference implementation\ndeliberately does not have. I had been corrected for exactly that twice the same\nday — inventing a validation scheme when the app makes bad values unenterable, and\nhanding mesh-gw two options I made up instead of the app\'s answer — and I did it\nagain within the hour.\n\nThen, when Peter said it would never work, I asked him to choose between "cut back"\nand "revert outright". A menu. He replied "how does phone app handle it. that will\nalways be my reply", which is the third correction of the same behaviour in one\nday. The answer was never his to give.\n\nThe memory how-does-the-phone-app-handle-it existed BEFORE this exchange — I wrote\nit hours earlier — and I still did not consult the app before designing. Writing\nthe rule down is not the same as applying it.\n\nWHAT SURVIVES FROM b038ccf: nothing of the mechanism, but the investigation was\nnot wasted — it is what found that saveOwner and saveFixedPosition never re-read\nat all, and it is why the three buttons no longer claim a proof we cannot perform.\nThe audit chain (a backup before every save, diffed) remains the thing that CAN\nprove what was written, done after the fact against a settled device rather than\n200ms after a write against a cache.\n\nSTILL OPEN, unchanged by this: the two legacy "verified" claims (config restore,\npushFixedPosition) that trust mesh-gw\'s old cooked-PUT flag. Ledger.'}
